= Part VI. The Sunnah and the Hadith

== 33. The authority of the Sunnah and the Quran-only claim

=== 33.1 The Quran and the Hadith: nature and status
// NODE P06.C01.S01
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML163:429-450 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("Whatever is in the Quran is not the statements of Prophet Muhammad. It's the word of God. We separate between what Allah says, which is all the Quran (all of it is just what Allah says, the word of God), and then the Hadith is what the Prophet taught and preached and expounded on. So unlike the Bible, we don't mix everyone's words with each other: Paul's words with Matthew, with Luke, with Jesus, with God. The Quran is just God's words only.", "The definition of a Hadith is what the Prophet said, did or agreed upon. What he told the people, expounding on the Quran, explaining verses of it: that's what the Hadith is. It's a Prophetic tradition essentially.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Baha'i Lady Questions Muslim On The Last Prophet! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5_h1_yrAXLg", ((1347, "22:27–22:53"), (1396, "23:16–23:32"),), id: 2002)
// U ML127:501-534 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("The verses are the Quran; the Hadith are not verses. They're just tradition. So we call it Ahadith. For Muslims, the Quran is the Word of God, verbatim: verbatim God speaking. The Hadith is not the verbatim Word of God. The Quran is, of course, more holy, because the Quran is the verbatim word of God. It's the speech of Allah.", "Now the Hadith is also a Revelation, but it's a Revelation to the Prophet, and the Prophet expresses it in his words, or events that take place where the Prophet agrees upon, or disagrees on, or things like that. So it's not the same as the Quran, of course. But again, I am 100% with the Hadith, the same way I'm 100% with the Quran.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Researcher Confronts Muslim On Extremist Teachings! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=G5Mgp9SzUc0", ((1359, "22:39–23:06"), (1388, "23:08–23:21"),), id: 2003)
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR002:1-20 | Farid Responds | 3
#passage(("I want to share some thoughts on a misconception that doesn't really get addressed much when we talk about Hadith rejection, and it has to do with the topic of Qur'an versus Hadith. This is how this sort of thing is usually viewed by the Hadith rejector: you got the Qur'an, and you got the Hadith. Why would I choose this if I have this? The Qur'an is a book from Allah subhanahu wa ta'ala, and the Hadith is not a book from Allah subhanahu wa ta'ala. This one here is by Imam Malik, and therefore we should accept the Qur'an, and we should reject the Hadith.", "I think the problem here is that the term Hadith is what confuses Hadith rejectors. The term Hadith makes it seem like this is a body of text that is similar to the Qur'an, that's competing with the Qur'an. I'm not going to attempt to redefine terms, but I'm just going to try to clarify terms. This book right here, much of it is historical reports, including the fiqhi reports, including jurisprudential rulings. They fall under history, and it's through knowing the history of Islam that you can fully appreciate this book.", "Of course, keep in mind that a book like Muwatta Malik or even Sahih Bukhari, these are not encompassing all of Islamic history, but these are texts written by respected classical scholars that put together compilations of reports, and there are many more than just those two. We're not dealing with Quran versus Hadith here. We're dealing with Quran versus history. So how exactly are we going to approach the Quran without history?",), "Farid Responds, “Quran VS Hadith”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9eBzpof1AQE", ((2, "0:02–0:46"), (48, "0:48–1:39"), (101, "1:41–2:13"),), id: 2004)
// U FR002:64-91 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("Why are books of Hadith seen as a competing text, or competing texts? The issue is with something else entirely. It's mainly to do with the laws. Hadith rejectors are going to look at this and they're going to see a book, and because it's a book, it's a competing text, and that's how comparisons and contrasting is made. However, if you think about it rationally, Hadiths, they're not [lengthy] pieces of text. In most cases we're dealing with a few sentences. What occurs is these compilers, someone as early as Malik, is approaching a narration that he received from one of his teachers and he's including in his book. Many cases, it's one sentence or two sentences. Malik is not even an author of a book. What he is is a compiler. The information is readily available in his society, and he's just gathering that information, putting them in one place.", "Some of you folks are saying: these books have rulings, and that's why I have an issue with them. If you're someone that uses a Hadith to better understand the context of the Quran, to better understand the environment, the lifestyle, then alhamdulillah, you're in a better place. You're not on the status of the extreme Hadith rejector that doubts everything in every historical work and every Hadith work, and that would be the most extreme type of Hadith rejector. And they're the ones that sound the most ridiculous, because who in their right mind would have an issue with a Hadith about the prophet, peace be upon him, eating chicken? There are rulings that are not found in the Quran itself. Some are alluded to by the Quran, and some are just impossible to reject historically.",), "Farid Responds, “Quran VS Hadith”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9eBzpof1AQE", ((342, "5:42–6:50"), (414, "6:54–8:05"),), id: 2005)
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML302:2-58 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("Now the Hadith is what Allah revealed to Prophet Muhammad that he commanded the people to do and prohibited people from doing. If the Quran has all the commands, it would be a massive book, because there's tens of thousands of Ahadeeth.", "So what the Hadith is: the Hadith is expounding on the Quran, the teachings of the Quran. For example, the Quran says \"pray\". It doesn't give you specified times of the day, doesn't give you specified units of the prayer. The Prophetic Tradition, through the Hadith, gives you the amount of Raka'at (amount of units) that you would do in Dhuhr, and what type of prayers are there, 5 times and all of that. So it gives you the specifications of the Commandments in the Quran. And the Quran itself tells you that: it tells you the Prophet is going to explain, he's going to teach you wisdom and he's going to give you Dhikr, i.e. Revelation that will explain the Quran.", "Now the number of Ahadith are things that are narrated from the Prophet. The Prophet had thousand of Companions; they heard what the Prophetﷺ said and they transmitted it to their Community. So it's not different authors; it's different people transmitting from the same source, which is the Prophet, what he said. So imagine: you will go transmit what I said and she will go transmit what I said; it's two different people transmitting information from one Source.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Visitor Questions Muslim On Hadith! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aVaXUL-Us6s", ((56, "0:56–1:12"), (83, "1:23–1:48"), (115, "1:55–2:10"),), id: 2006)
// U ML058:299-361 | The Muslim Lantern | 4
#passage(("The Quran is not Prophet Muhammad's speech. Now, a lot of people misunderstand this. They look at the Bible, for example. The Bible is inspired word of God: means those people wrote their own words and they were inspired by God. No. The Quran is the first person words of God. It's not inspired words of Prophet Muhammad from God. It is God himself literally in the first person speaking, and that then transmitted to Prophet Muhammad. Prophet Muhammad is repeating the literal words of God to the people.", "Now, we have a separate tradition which we call the Hadith. Here's where the inspiration part comes: Prophet would be inspired to say certain things. Prophet even told us there are certain things the prophet would do that are not to do with delivering the message. These can be his human things. These are not binding for us. So the Hadith contains both. So, that which is legally binding for us is that which is explained in the Quran or the prophet explains it to us as a legally binding thing: God commanded us to do this, do this, don't do that, as legally binding things.", "Now, things for example that are worldly things, like things that you do in the culture, for example how you dress, how prophet Muhammad dress: I'm not obliged as a Muslim to dress as prophet Muhammad. What if the prophet is talking about things in this life? Like he told some people some things to do with farming, and then they came and they told him this did not work out that way. He said to them: you are more knowledgeable in the things to do with your worldly things. So, farming and these things, you're more knowledgeable there.", "So, there is a clear separation when it comes to the teachings regarding the Sharia, the laws, the specific teachings of the do's and don'ts. And then there's things that the prophet would do that are outside that; that's not legally binding for us, if the prophet does something that is not within the corpus of direct revelation from God as a command to deliver to us as people. So, Allah says in the Quran he doesn't speak of his own, as revelation given to him, meaning when he gives you the message, when he gives you things explaining the Quran or legal rulings that you can or cannot do. These things are legally binding for us.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Agnostic Claims That Pantheism Is More Rational Than Monotheism! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=C3BXt0zJr3o", ((1068, "17:48–18:17"), (1118, "18:38–19:16"), (1158, "19:18–19:44"), (1188, "19:48–20:24"),), id: 2007)
// U ML224:266-280 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("Hadith is the narration of the prophet, and then we have a historical process of authenticating it. If it's an [authentically] attributed statement to the prophet, then it is binding, because Allah told you in the Quran he speaks Revelation not of his own, and that follow the prophet, and whatever he tells you to do, do, and whatever he tells you not to do, do not do. How do you actually apply in reality? The prophet is the example of how to do it.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Agnostic Man Grills Muslim With Difficult Questions! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-rSkeAMTyK0", ((1041, "17:21–17:56"),), id: 2008)

=== 33.2 The Quranic basis for following the Prophet
// NODE P06.C01.S02
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML294:1-265 | The Muslim Lantern | 19
#passage(("A specific group of people who like to name themselves \"Quranists\" or \"Quran Alone\" or whatever it is, when they are in reality just what we call \"Hadith Rejectors\": they are a group of people who are spreading today. They are spreading amongst the ignorant Muslims, people who have not studied their religion, they've not studied Hadith, they've not studied the Quran, who are not aware of Usool أصول (the Fundamental Principles) that a person should have as a Muslim. And they're also spreading among the Reverts (people who are new Muslims), and the reason they are spreading among reverts: because some of these reverts, they come from a Christian background and have a skepticism towards the Bible. So they tend to compare the Bible with the Hadith, and then they come to this kind of fear that: \"Just like the Bible has been corrupted, perhaps the Hadith has been corrupted as well.\" And then they seep into using their doubts and manipulate those individuals, because they're not aware even of what Hadith is and what is the history of Hadith or any of that.", "And it's mainly one of two reasons why a person would become a Hadith rejecter and rejects Hadith. Either it's complete ignorance, meaning that he came across something, came across Ahadith, came across a doubt, and he could not find an answer for it due to his ignorance; he completely decided to reject Hadith as a wholesale, or reject a group of Ahadith he doesn't like, because he couldn't find an understanding for these Ahadith. So that's one reason. And the other reason is desires: people who want to interpret the Quran the way they want, who don't want to follow a specific kind of teachings in the Hadith. They want to do things their own way, and because of their own desires and wanting to avoid all of these different types of things, they end up in the end rejecting Hadith and pretending that they are following the Quran.", "And the interesting thing about this group of people is the fact that the same Hadith that they deny has prophesied their coming, and the Prophetﷺ mentioned that this group of people will come. So us, as Muslims who follow the Quran, who follow the Hadith, as any Muslim should be, we're not really surprised by their existence; they're not really a new group of people coming. But there is a need for clarification for the individuals and the people who don't understand who these people are.", "So the Prophetﷺ, in that Hadith which they themselves reject, he said: يوشِكُ أنْ يقعُدَ الرجلُ مُتَّكِئًا على أَرِيكَتِهِ (There will come a time when you'll find that a man reclining on his couch..). Why did the Prophetﷺ say reclining on his couch? There are many reasons why the Prophetﷺ said that. The Prophetﷺ is alluding to his arrogance, for example, the way he's reclining on his couch like the kings used to recline on their couches. So he's implying that this person has arrogance in his heart. He's also implying the reality that this individual has not done much work; he's just reclining, relaxing. He thinks he knows what he's doing, he thinks he understands what he's talking about.", "And then him reclining on his couch, what is he going to say? يُحَدَّثُ بحديثٍ مِنْ حديثي (So someone will narrate to him a Hadith from my Hadith). So someone will narrate something about me, which is basically what Hadith is; so someone will mention a Hadith of the Prophetﷺ. فيقولُ Then this man who's sitting on the couch, what is he going to say? بينَنَا وبينَكُمْ كتابُ اللهِ (Between us and you is the book of Allah) فما وجدْنا فيه مِنْ حلالٍ اسْتَحْلَلْناهُ (What we find in it Halal, we take it as Halal, and what we find in it Haram, we say it's Haram then). And then the Prophetﷺ responded to them: ألَا وإِنَّ ما حرَّمَ رسولُ اللهِ مثلَ ما حرَّمَ اللهُ (That which the Messenger of Allahﷺ says is Haram, it is the exact same as if Allah is telling you it is Haram). It is the same thing. Why? Because, as Allahﷻ says in the Quran (Annajm53:3-4): وَمَا يَنطِقُ عَنِ الْهَوَىٰ*إِنْ هُوَ إِلَّا وَحْيٌ يُوحَى (He does not speak of his own, rather it's Revelation from Allah coming down to the Prophetﷺ).", "So those individuals, they've been already spoken about by the same Hadith, which is what people would refer to as irony, even though that term is not necessarily correct. But this is the reality: they are rejecting the Hadith, and the Hadith is exactly describing them.", "Also 'Umar ibn alKhattab, he alluded to these individuals by saying that there was the verse of رجم (Rajm: stoning), the verse that talks about stoning the adulterers (persons who commits adultery) in the Quran, and this verse was uplifted. It was abrogated, as the Quran mentions in many places that there is abrogation. So that verse was abrogated, and then 'Umar ibn Alkhattab said that (the Prophetﷺ, he stoned a person who committed adultery) after the verse was abrogated, and then he said (Abu Bakr did as well), and then he said (and we did as well). And then he says (and I'm afraid that if time goes by, a group of people will come and they're going to say), exactly what those Hadith rejecters say today, they will say: (that we don't find stoning in the Quran). فيضلُّوا بتركِ فريضةٍ أنْزلَها الله تعالى (So they become misguided by leaving an obligation that Allahﷻ has revealed).", "So 'Umar ibn Alkhattab here is exactly talking about these group of people. You'll find these group of people completely denying stoning and say that stoning is not in the Quran, this punishment is barbaric. And they're influenced by liberalism and this and that, and their desires, and many reasons that we already mentioned. But you see how the Hadith, the same Hadith that they reject, completely speaks about the reality, and speaks about what they will do in the future, which we can see and observe with our own eyes. So this is a sign for us, for people who accept the Hadith and people who understand the historicity of the Hadith, that these people are not new people that are coming, but these people have been foretold by the Prophetﷺ, and even spoken about by his Companions, the likes of 'Umar ibn Alkhattab, who was second Caliphate of Islam.", "The first argument I want to present is a simple idea; this idea is that the Quran mentions certain information. This certain information has been mentioned by the Prophetﷺ in the Quran: so Allah quotes the Prophetﷺ saying certain things in the Quran. So first I want to establish a general rule and a principle that, as Hadith rejecters, they have to accept, because it's in the Quran. Allahﷻ says about the Prophetﷺ (Alhaqqa69:44-46): وَلَوْ تَقَوَّلَ عَلَيْنَا بَعْضَ الْأَقَاوِيلِ* لَأَخَذْنَا مِنْهُ بِالْيَمِينِ * ثُمَّ لَقَطَعْنَا مِنْهُ الْوَتِينَ (If the Prophetﷺ were to make up certain statements and lies about Allahﷻ, Allahﷻ is going to seize him..), meaning that he is going to die. Allahﷻ is going to cause him to die and is going to punish him. So that means that the Prophetﷺ has no possibility of making up information about Allahﷻ. I'm sure us and the Hadith rejecters agree on that, but they completely deny any information from him anyways. But we agree on the principle that the Prophetﷺ cannot make up statements about Allah, because if he did he would be dead, based on that verse of Quran that you accept; and we already mentioned that the Prophetﷺ, what he speaks is Revelation anyway.", "So Allahﷻ says in the Quran a statement about the Prophetﷺ in chapter:3 (Al'imran) of the Quran, verse:124. Allahﷻ says: إِذْ تَقُولُ لِلْمُؤْمِنِينَ أَلَن يَكْفِيَكُمْ أَن يُمِدَّكُمْ رَبُّكُم بِثَلَاثَةِ آلَافٍ مِّنَ الْمَلَائِكَةِ مُنزَلِينَ. Allah is saying: (When you said to the believers, O'Prophet: isn't it sufficient for you that Allah is going to give you 3000 angels descending?) Now my question is to those Hadith rejecters who only pretend the only Revelation that was revealed is the Quran, and there's no other sources of Revelation, and all the Hadith are not reliable, and we don't accept information from the Hadith, or we don't accept history and all of that; it's only the Quran, the Quran alone, is the only thing you accept. Now the question is: where did Allahﷻ say to the Prophetﷺ that Allah is going to enforce him with 3000 angels? Find me a verse in the Quran where Allahﷻ before told the Prophetﷺ that he's going to enforce him with 3000 angels, then the Prophetﷺ went and told this information to the believers. Because if that information is not in the Quran, and the Quran is the only Revelation, then where did the Prophetﷺ get this information, that there is 3000 angels that Allah is going to enforce the believers with? You've got no option except to accept that there is another Revelation revealed to Prophetﷺ, where he was told that he will be enforced by 3000 angels.", "There's many examples. Second example is: Allahﷻ, for example, says in the Quran chapter:2 (Albaqara), verse:142: سَيَقُولُ السُّفَهَاءُ مِنَ النَّاسِ مَا وَلَّاهُمْ عَن قِبْلَتِهِمُ (The ignorant of the people, they will say: why did they move away from their Qibla (direction of prayer)?) Then Allahﷻ says, moving forward, in the verse:143: وَمَا جَعَلْنَا الْقِبْلَةَ الَّتِي كُنتَ عَلَيْهَا إِلَّا لِنَعْلَمَ مَن يَتَّبِعُ الرَّسُولَ مِمَّن يَنقَلِبُ عَلَىٰ عَقِبَيْهِ. So Allahﷻ says: (we made) وَمَا جَعَلْنَا. And جَعَلْنَا here is referring to Allah. Allah is The One Who \"made\", according to the Quran: (Allah made the previous Qibla as a test to see who will follow the Messenger and who will turn on his back). So now the question is: in this verse, Allah says direct yourself to Mecca, but Allah also says in the same verse that he made the previous Qibla a direction; He's The One Who made it. Now find me in the Quran where is this command to follow the first Qibla, the Qibla before Mecca. Where is that command in the Quran? Because Allahﷻ is saying we made the previous Qibla, which means that the command to pray to a different Qibla before Mecca was from Allah. If you cannot find it, then, if you're a sincere person and you have any intellect, then you'd have to admit that there is another source of Revelation other than the Quran, where this information was, by Revelation, conveyed to the Prophetﷺ, not through the Quran, but through another source of Revelation.", "The 3rd example I'm going to give for this specific argument is: Allahﷻ says in the Quran chapter:2 (Albaqara), verse:187: أُحِلَّ لَكُمْ لَيْلَةَ الصِّيَامِ الرَّفَثُ إِلَىٰ نِسَائِكُمْ (Allahﷻ has now made it permissible for you to have intercourse with your women in the night of fasting). In the nights of Ramadan, the nights of fasting, you can have intercourse with your women. And Allah says that He knows that you used to betray yourselves: عَلِمَ اللَّهُ أَنَّكُمْ كُنتُمْ تَخْتَانُونَ أَنفُسَكُمْ فَتَابَ عَلَيْكُمْ وَعَفَا عَنكُمْ (So he forgives you for disobeying his command and betraying yourselves in the past, so now..) فَالْآنَ بَاشِرُوهُنَّ (So now you're allowed to be intimate with them). Where is that command where Allahﷻ told them that they cannot have intercourse with them in the night? Because Allah is saying now you can; it has become Halal (permissible) for you now. If you cannot find that command in the Quran, then you have to accept the reality, the fact, that there is another source of Revelation other than the Quran. So that is the first argument that we have, which is a positive argument for the Hadith, which shows that there is another Revelation other than the Quran.", "The second argument that we're going to talk about is that the Quran speaks about something that it calls \"wisdom\" حكمة (Hikma). Hikma in the Arabic language is referred to as wisdom; this is the most common meaning of the word Hikma. So Allahﷻ speaks about something that is called Hikma in the Quran.", "I want to talk about something very important to keep in mind. In the Arabic language, and even in the English language and other languages, you have a word that is used for conjunction between two words. Like for example: \"I like bananas and oranges\". So when you say \"and\", you use it to have a conjunction between two things that are different from each other. You don't say: \"I ate pineapple and pineapple\". This is in English, and it's the same in Arabic: when you say وَ (Wa), like قابلتُ زيداً وعمرو (I met Zayd and 'Amr), you do not say in Arabic: \"I met Zaid and Zaid\" قابلتُ زيداً وزيداً. So the letter و (wa), which is translated into \"and\" in English, is used for conjunction. It is used for two things that are different from one another, because what those Hadith rejecters tend to do is that they throw around all logic, and they try to pretend that the word \"and\" is speaking about the same thing.", "So Allahﷻ says in the Quran, in multiple different places, that He's revealed something called Alhikma. So that Hikma is Revelation from Allahﷻ, and that Hikma is not the Quran; it's something other than the Quran. Why is it something other than the Quran? You look at the Quran chapter:2 of the Quran, verse:231: وَمَا أَنزَلَ عَلَيْكُم مِّنَ الْكِتَابِ وَالْحِكْمَةِ (And what He revealed to you from the Kitab \"the book\" (i.e. the Quran), and the Hikma). So here we have two things: we have the Kitab, which is the Quran, and the Hikma, and both of them are Revelation. Allah is saying وَمَا أَنزَلَ عَلَيْكُم (What He revealed to you). In another place as well, in chapter:4 (Annisa') of the Quran, verse:113, Allah speaks directly to the Prophet; he says: وَأَنزَلَ اللَّهُ عَلَيْكَ الْكِتَابَ وَالْحِكْمَةَ (Allah revealed to you, O'Prophet, Alkitab (i.e Quran), and the Hikma). Alkitab here is the Quran, because that is what was revealed to the Prophetﷺ or upon the Prophetﷺ, and the Hikma.", "So we have something called Hikma that's revealed from Allah, and we'd like those Hadith rejecters to tell us: what is this Hikma? Why do they have to tell us what is this Hikma? Because that Hikma is not only Revelation from Allah, but it's also something that is being taught to the believers. And who is teaching it? It is the Prophetﷺ. And it cannot be the Quran, because we already said that Allah mentioned that it is something else other than the Quran. Allahﷻ says in the Quran, commanding the Prophetﷺ or mentioning to us (as believers), in chapter:2 (Albaqara), verse:129: وَيُعَلِّمُهُمُ الْكِتَابَ وَالْحِكْمَةَ (that Messenger will teach us the Book (the Quran) and the Hikma). So that Hikma, which is revelation from Allah, is going to be taught by the Messenger, and it is not the Quran. So my question is now for those Hadith rejectors: where is that Hikma that is being taught by the Prophetﷺ? Where can we find it? In which book is it? Because it's other than the Quran: وَيُعَلِّمُهُمُ الْكِتَابَ, He already (teaches them the Quran), so you cannot say it is the Quran. وَالْحِكْمَةَ.", "And that is mentioned in many verses of the Quran, like (Albaqara2:151) (teaches them the Quran (Kitab) and the Hikma), and many verses of the Quran; chapter:3 (Al'imran), verse:164, again saying that: (He teaches them his verses and he teaches them the Kitab (Quran) and he teaches them the Hikma). So the Hikma is other than the Kitab; that is something that is being taught by the Prophetﷺ. It is Revelation from Allah. Not only that, it is something that is recited or said out loud, because Allahﷻ says in the Quran chapter:33 (Alahzab), verse:34: وَاذْكُرْنَ مَا يُتْلَىٰ فِي بُيُوتِكُنَّ مِنْ آيَاتِ اللَّهِ وَالْحِكْمَةِ (And mention and recite what you hear in your houses from the verses of Allah, which is the Quran, and the Hikma). So what is this Hikma? What is this Revelation taught by the Prophetﷺ and being recited out loud, and it is not the Quran?", "As أهل السنة (Ahl Assunnah: People of the Sunnah), we say that the Hikma is clearly, without a shadow of a doubt, the Hadith, the explanations of the Prophetﷺ which expounds on the Quran. And you see that mentioned very clearly in chapter:16 (Annahl) of the Quran, verse:44, where Allahﷻ says: وَأَنزَلْنَا إِلَيْكَ الذِّكْرَ (So we revealed to you the Dhikr); Dhikr here we say it is the Sunnah. لِتُبَيِّنَ لِلنَّاسِ مَا نُزِّلَ إِلَيْهِمْ (So you can explain to the people that which was revealed to them before). So there is something that was revealed to the people that the Prophetﷺ is going to explain using another Revelation. So there's two Revelations there: وَأَنزَلْنَا إِلَيْكَ الذِّكْرَ / مَا نُزِّلَ إِلَيْهِمْ. There's two things here mentioned: Dhikr is mentioned, and something else that was revealed. We say that something else that was revealed is the Quran, and Dhikr here is the Hikma, the Sunnah, that the Prophetﷺ is going to use to explain to the people.", "But regardless whether you, as a Hadith rejector, want to give the interpretation of the Dhikr or not, you want to accept this as the Sunnah or not, or you want to say that the Hikma is the Sunnah or not, you still have a problem to deal with. And that problem is that all of these verses are mentioning something other than the Quran, and it is saying that the Prophetﷺ is teaching it, and it is for us as believers. So the question is for you and me as a believer now: where do we find the other thing that the prophet is teaching? If you don't want to tell us what the Hikma is, you can explain to us: what is that thing that the Prophetﷺ is going to teach, that is other than the Quran?",), "The Muslim Lantern, “A Complete Annihilation Of Hadith Rejector's Methodology! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1Gc0mbEqasg", ((4, "0:04–0:50"), (57, "0:57–1:31"), (96, "1:36–1:55"), (120, "2:00–2:30"), (153, "2:33–3:22"), (207, "3:27–3:36"), (232, "3:52–4:34"), (281, "4:41–5:15"), (438, "7:18–8:12"), (497, "8:17–9:31"), (585, "9:45–11:10"), (673, "11:13–12:17"), (741, "12:21–12:35"), (758, "12:38–13:32"), (825, "13:45–14:24"), (871, "14:31–15:15"), (926, "15:26–16:05"), (974, "16:14–16:52"), (1018, "16:58–17:39"),), id: 2009)
