#passage(("The third argument is quite basic. That argument is a basic, clear cut command in the Quran, and it will come back to a point that we mentioned as well, and that point is the word \"and\", which is a conjunction between two different things. There are tens of verses in the Quran, and you can even say hundreds if you want to combine just the meaning of obedience towards Allah and the Messenger in the Quran. Allahﷻ says in the Quran (Alimran3:132): (Obey Allah and the Messenger that you may attain peace). Allahﷻ says (Annisa4:59): (O' who you believe, obey Allah and obey the Messenger). Allahﷻ says (Almaida5:92): (And obey Allah and obey the Messenger). Allahﷻ says (Alanfal8:20): (Obey Allah and His Messenger). Allahﷻ says (Alanfal8:46): (Obey Allah and His Messenger). Allahﷻ says (Annoor24:54): (Say: obey Allah and obey the Messenger). Allahﷻ says (Annoor24:56): (Just obey the Messenger): the Messenger alone here, without even obeying Allah. Allahﷻ says (Muhammad47:33): (O' you who believe, obey Allah and obey the Messenger). Allahﷻ says (Attaghabun64:12): (Obey Allah and obey the Messenger).", "These are all different verses that are all essentially saying obey Allah and obey the Messenger, or obey Allah and His Messenger, and there are many verses. And they're mentioned in different wordings, like for example: (Whoever obeys Allah and His Messenger, He will admit him into paradise) (Annisa4:13); (And who obeys the Messenger has obeyed Allah) (Annisa4:80); (And who obeys Allah and the Messenger.. he will be from those that Allah has bestowed favor upon) (Annisa4:69).", "When you speak to Hadith rejectors, they try to pretend the word \"and\" doesn't mean \"and\", and it is not a conjunction between 2 things, so it's not like the obedience of Allah is different from the obedience of the Messenger. We would say that makes zero sense, for many reasons. First, we would say that would render the meaning of the words of Allah meaningless, because essentially, the Hadith rejectors say, when Allah says obey the Messenger, it is referring to the Quran or it's referring to obeying Allah. So then we would say that renders the Quran meaningless. Why? Because it would mean that all of these verses are saying obey Allah and obey Allah, obey Allah and obey Allah. So that is the meaning of the verses according to those Hadith rejectors, and these are tens of verses! And all of them mention the Messenger by name, and some of them just mention the Messengerﷺ without even mentioning Allah; some of them saying obeying the Messenger is obedience to Allah, which means clearly that there are 2 types of different obedience.", "So, 1st, it renders verses of Allah meaningless. 2nd, it opposes language, when you have a conjunction between two words. And 3rd, it just shows that the Quran according to them is not eloquent, because there are repetition of words without a necessity to repeat them. If someone else was revealing the Quran, it would be easier and more eloquent for him to just mention obey Allah, because according to them, if you obey the Messenger, it means to obey Allah, so it means the Quran. So then Allah saying obey Allah is enough; he doesn't need to say obey the Messenger. But in reality, all of these verses are like a thorn in their throats. They know that these are two different types of obediences; that's why Allahﷻ mentions two types of obediences. He doesn't just say obey Allah and His Messenger, but He says obey Allah \"and obey\". So, He adds the word \"obey\" specifically for the Messenger to show that these are two different types of obediences, which shows the importance of listening and obeying to the Messengerﷺ in that which he will be teaching. And where is that thing that the Prophetﷺ is teaching? Because the Prophet is sent to everyone; he's not just sent to his Companions. So me and you and all the believers need to find that thing, and that is, as we say, of course the Hadith.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “A Complete Annihilation Of Hadith Rejector's Methodology! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1Gc0mbEqasg", ((1063, "17:43–18:54"), (1140, "19:00–19:17"), (1168, "19:28–20:13"), (1219, "20:19–21:20"),), id: 2011)
