// U FR106:1-29 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("Some may ask the question: why aren't all the rulings in the Quran? Instead you have a lot of rulings in the Hadith. And why do I need to follow all these extra rulings, because they're not in the book of Allah; if they were as important, then they would have been in the book of Allah. Well, you got to keep a few things in mind. Firstly, the main purpose of the Quran is to provide you a general guidance in terms of your connection to Allah, why you're here, where you're going. What you do not have, of course, is a detailed list of every single action. The Quran, of course, is written in a very poetic, eloquent way, and if you had a list of dos and don'ts, that would simply take away from the poetic nature of the Quran. And due to that, you simply have the Quran speaking in general about some orders and some prohibitions, and then you have the prophet, peace be upon Him, expounding upon those and going into detail about those specific matters.",), "Farid Responds, “Are Hadiths Necessary? Isn't the Quran Enough?”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6CA1zIFU8cM", ((7, "0:07–1:18"),), id: 2018)
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML294:322-421 | The Muslim Lantern | 8
#passage(("The next argument I want to talk about is the argument of generalities. What is the argument of generalities? It is the argument that the verses in the Quran, many of those verses of the Quran, are quite general. They're not specific, but rather they're general in their commands. And the question is to those Hadith rejectors: how do we understand these verses? Who helps us to understand how to perform or do these commands that are found in the Quran? According to whose understanding should we be following regarding these verses or these commands in the Quran? And the thing is, when you look at these verses in the Quran, these commands can be understood in hundreds of different ways, depending on the person's intellect and depending on what games he wants to play with the Arabic language and whatever he wants.", "So according to us, we say that Hadith will restrict those meanings, if it does not give you the right meaning, the only meaning: it restricts those meanings to 2 or 3 different meanings, for example, in which all are accurate. So the Hadith is explaining and expounding on Quran, and giving you details that are missing, that you do not find in these verses, and gives you the understanding of generalities that you find in these verses, how to completely understand and implement them. While when you look at the Hadith rejectors, they have no way of understanding them. We need the example of the Prophetﷺ in order for us to follow the Prophetﷺ, and for us to understand those general commands that are found in the Quran.", "And I'll give an example: Praying Salat صلاة. How do we know how to pray Salat? And you'll find a lot of Hadith rejectors saying praying the Salat has been passed down through التواتر العمَلي (Mass-Transmission in actions), which is the actions of the people: that's how we know how we pray, we don't need anything. The Prophetﷺ prayed and people saw him, how he prayed, and then they passed it onto the next generation, and this is how we know how to pray. So we don't need the Hadith for that. Some Hadith rejectors will make this argument, but that argument is still faulty, because the question is: where did the Prophet get how to pray from? Because according to you, the Quran is the only Revelation, so did he make up how to pray? We already established that he cannot make up how to pray. How did he know how to pray, for people to imitate him generation after generation, today for us to know how to pray? So your understanding will still remain to be faulty, saying that anyone can make up any way of how to pray, like certain individuals: I'll pray whatever way you want to pray, pray anytime you want to pray, any amount of ركعات (Raka'at: prayer Units) I want to pray, according to their own understanding of the prayer.", "And why are we saying the verses of the Quran can be completely general, if you want to generalise them? I'll give an example. When Allah says in the Quran (Albaqarah2:43) وَأَقِيمُوا الصَّلَاةَ (establish Salat): what is Salat? What does Salat mean? Someone will say: \"Salat is to pray\". So how do you pray? Someone says: \"Salat means to raise your hand and make دعاء Dua'a (Supplication) to Allah\", and this and that, \"because in the Arabic language you use the word \"Salat\" and \"Dua'a\" in conjunction; Salat here actually means Dua'a, so it means to supplicate. So, when Allah says establish the Salat, it means supplicate to Allah.\" OK, that's one person who is going to come and say that. Another person is going to say: \"No, the word \"Salat\" actually comes from the word صِلة in the Arabic language, which means a connection. So it means to connect to Allah. It does not actually mean pray by raising your hands and performing those actions.\" Then you find another Hadith rejecter that says: \"No, actually Salat, it's actually prostrating and this and that, but do it according to my way, because I understood this verse this way, so this is how you should do it!\" And then every Tom, Dick, and Harry comes and brings his own understanding.", "For example, Allah says in the Quran (AlBaqara2:228) (the divorced women, they have to wait ثَلَاثَةَ قُرُوءٍ). The word قرء in the Arabic language has two opposite meanings: one meaning is \"purity\", and one meaning is \"menstruation\". So these are two meanings in the Arabic language. The people of Hijaz, the region of Hijaz, understand it to mean Purity, and the people of Iraq understand it to mean menstruation. Now according to Hadith rejectors, when he divorces his women, some Hadith rejectors will say it means this, and other Hadith rejectors will say it means that. But the Hadith helps you to understand which one is it, using the examples of the Prophet, used in other examples. But the point is: which one are you going to take, if you are a Hadith rejecter, if you completely have no authority for the Hadith? Because we can say there are two meanings, and the two meanings we can even say they're valid based on the Hadith. But you: which meaning is the correct meaning according to you, because the meanings are opposing each other, and you completely reject the Hadith?", "For example, Allah says in the Quran (Yusuf12:20): وَشَرَوْهُ بِثَمَنٍ بَخْسٍ (They sold him for a cheap price), about Yusuf 'alayhi salam (peace be upon him), but the word شَرَوْهُ here can mean they sold him, according to some Hadith rejectors, and it means they bought him, according to some others. If you just use the language, you will end up with all of these different meanings, and you'll end up confused, not knowing where to apply when and to apply what, according to whose understanding. Because language can give you tens of meanings, especially the Arabic language, for one word, and tens of understandings based on that, and tens of different groups following different things.", "When Allah says, the thief, the male and female, chapter:5 (alMaida5:38) فاقْطَعُوا أَيْدِيَهُمَا, about cutting: now, cutting, what does it mean according to some? According to some, cut your relationship with them, according to some Hadith rejecters that I heard. According to another Hadith rejecter it is this. What is the meaning? And is it if they steal anything? Is it if they steal at any time? Is it if they steal at any age? All of these restrictions that the Hadith gives you, the amount that they steal and how they steal and all of that, are not found in the Quran, which means now, according to you, if you see anyone steals anywhere, any amount, you should do قطع (cutting), and then you should explain to us first what قطع means. Does it mean cutting? Does it mean boycotting? What does it mean exactly?", "I can give hundreds of examples, showing that this idea of generality: by necessity you need someone who gives you a restriction for these verses that can be understood generally, and you can go to extremes in your understanding of the Quran regarding certain verses about fighting, for example, or attacking the disbelievers, or this or that. A person can easily go extreme by understanding them generally, without any restrictions of Hadith of whom you can attack, whom you cannot attack, and all of these other restrictions that we have. So, that is the argument: using the Quran, looking at it like this with generalities, is not going to help you, and you will end up creating hundreds of different sects, and that cannot be guidance, and Allahﷻ claims that the Quran is guidance.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “A Complete Annihilation Of Hadith Rejector's Methodology! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1Gc0mbEqasg", ((1286, "21:26–22:01"), (1327, "22:07–22:56"), (1382, "23:02–23:58"), (1442, "24:02–24:49"), (1492, "24:52–25:44"), (1544, "25:44–26:11"), (1575, "26:15–26:51"), (1615, "26:55–27:28"),), id: 2019)
