#passage(("A contradiction is two statements that cannot be reconciled. There are not two statements that cannot be reconciled: one thing is not in The Quran and it is in the Hadith. That does not mean it's a contradiction. So all what is going on there is there is extra information within the Hadith that is not in The Quran. We have to be very clear in the terms that we use. So if I say there is a contradiction between The Quran and The Hadith, this is a problem, because they're both supposed to come from the same Creator; but if I say something is in The Hadith, is not in The Quran, that's not an issue at all. Why? Because we believe that Prophet Muhammad, peace be upon him, he came and his mission is to explain The Quran, and also The Quran itself states that The Prophet speaks Revelation and he gets Revelation from God. It says, for example, in Surat An-Najm53:4-3: وَمَا يَنطِقُ عَنِ الْهَوَىٰ، إِنْ هُوَ إِلَّا وَحْيٌ يُوحَى. So The Prophetﷺ (does not speak of his own desires, does not speak of his own; it's only Revelation given to him).", "So when The Prophetﷺ says to us about stoning, which he himself applied to certain Companions when he was alive (he applied the punishment to Ma'iz and AlGhamidiya, which were two of the Companions of The Prophet; he applied the punishment to them), which shows that this punishment is revelation, comes from God. Now, does it have to be in The Quran? No! Not everything in The Sharia, in our tradition, our belief as Muslims, has to be in The Quran. If everything were in The Quran, The Quran will probably be thousands upon thousands upon thousands of words, and no one will ever be able to read it. So if The Quran has all of the descriptions of the prayer, if The Quran has all of the descriptions of The Zakah, if The Quran has all of the descriptions of Sawm \"Fasting\", if The Quran has all of the descriptions of Wudu' (ablution) and what you can do, what breaks your Wudu', the pillars of the prayer... All of these things which are in books of Fiqh, and all of these extra things which The Prophetﷺ told us about, the exact times of the prayers, the exact units \"Rakaat\", how many units you do when you pray: The Prophetﷺ is the one who explains these things. The Prophetﷺ is the one who tells us the exact extra details of these issues. So not everything is in The Quran; Muslims do not believe everything is in The Quran. So yes, there are teachings that are in The Hadith but not in The Quran. We have no issues with them, and stoning the adultress is one of them.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Coptic Christian Questions Muslim On Hadith! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5FilpKrRCFE", ((181, "3:01–3:53"), (241, "4:01–5:10"),), id: 2020)
// R quran_053_003-004.json
