// U FR002:36-63 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("This whole Quran versus history thing, it really doesn't make much sense. You cannot separate the Quran from history. Imagine that a Christian is going through the Bible and is just going to go through the red-letter text (those are like the words of Jesus), and all the other stuff in the Gospel a Christian is going to reject, and they're going to say: \"No, that's just history. I'm not going to trust that sort of thing.\" A Christian realizes that they need to accept the history of Jesus in order to believe in Jesus. You can't believe in something that you know pretty much nothing about.", "Of course, there is a little issue with the analogy, and I'm sure the Hadith rejecters here may have noticed. Yes, there are people that have listened to the Quran and have converted to Islam just by listening to the Quran, realizing this from Allah Subhana Wa Ta'ala. They get really affected by the meanings, despite not knowing anything more about the religion apart from this book, apart from the words in the book. The message of the Quran is so powerful that Subhanallah people are just entranced by it, and it's very strange that they do not need a context in order for them to believe in the book. So, I will concede that this is a reality, and I personally can't imagine being in the shoes of someone that would go so far that they would say: \"Yes, I accept everything in the book, but I reject the history that's tied to the book.\" Because you're missing a large part of the puzzle. The amount of information that we miss out on that has to do with the lives of the companions and the life of the Prophet, peace be upon him, their society, the history of wars, the alliances, all of that is going to be dismissed. Why? Because you have folks with this mentality that's like Quran versus Hadith. And again, Hadith rejecters are approaching this all wrong, because these are not competing works. History cannot be disregarded when understanding a classical text. History provides us context to that text.",), "Farid Responds, “Quran VS Hadith”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9eBzpof1AQE", ((190, "3:10–3:57"), (237, "3:57–5:39"),), id: 2023)
// U FR106:74-118 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("If you're not familiar with what's going on at the time, if you're not familiar with their classical language, if you're not familiar with their Customs, a chunk of the Quran wouldn't make sense to you, because you're a 21st century individual living in a completely different world. One example is chapter 2:189, that says: don't enter houses from the back, and enter them from their doors. What's going on here? Well, back in the day, when people were going out to pilgrimage, what used to happen was they had the Superstition that you shouldn't enter your home from the front door if you forgot something. So what they do is they'd go from the back of their houses, and they would actually break through their houses, dig into their houses, in order to get whatever they forgot inside: some random weird superstition. And of course the Quran says that this is not something good, and stop doing it. So if you simply look at the Quran, if you read the verses, you'd have no idea, and what you had was second generation Muslims, you even have a companion for the prophet, speaking about what exactly happened, what exactly is behind those verses. And then of course several others, especially when it comes to [matters] to do with the pilgrimage: several verses that have to do with the pilgrimage, just by reading them, you have no idea what's going on.",), "Farid Responds, “Are Hadiths Necessary? Isn't the Quran Enough?”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6CA1zIFU8cM", ((226, "3:46–5:36"),), id: 2024)
