// U FR012:1-46 | Farid Responds | 5
#passage(("I've spoken to a lot of Hadith rejecters. I've spoken to casual Hadith rejecters, staunch Hadith rejecters, conspiratorial Hadith rejecters. I've spoken to Hadith rejecters that pray the way Sunnis do. And I've spoken to Hadith rejecters that deny that the Kaaba is in Mecca. I've spoken to all sorts. There are various types of Hadith rejecters online and in real life. You can't really paint all Hadith rejecters with one brush. However, many of them are sincere people that have gone astray due to some misinformation. So, the main thing that you got to keep in mind is you got to be patient with them.", "So, what exactly is happening here? Most Muslims are Muslims that are born to Islam. They're people that have a background of Hadith. And due to that, they are following Hadith up until one specific point where they decide to reject Hadith. Usually what happens is they come across narrations that they find problematic. And due to that, they struggle to deal with these narrations. And these are reports that would be highlighted by opposing sects, perhaps by critics of Islam. And in most cases, these are narrations that are connected to morality. And I often hear things like, \"There's no way that the prophet, peace be upon him, would have done this,\" or that he would have said this. And that's a very important statement that you need to keep in mind.", "Coming across one Hadith initially that's problematic isn't really going to take someone out of Sunnism. What usually happens is they come across the first and the second and then the third, and then by the time they get to the fourth they think, \"Why am I even following Hadith in the first place? Why do I even believe in this stuff?\" Now, the reason why they don't leave Islam altogether, or at least they don't claim to leave Islam altogether, is because they don't view the Quran in the same way. If they find something problematic in the Quran, they will try to interpret it in a way to fit in with their values, and it's sometimes easier to do that with the Quran because the Quran is not as rigid and as direct as the Hadith.", "So ultimately, what happens with Hadith rejecters is they hold onto the Quran alone and then they reject all Hadiths, or that's what they claim. And the reason why I'm saying that is because the vast majority of Hadith rejecters, they unknowingly accept Hadiths which affect the way they view the prophet, peace be upon him. When they come across a Hadith they don't like, they will say, \"There's no way that the prophet, peace be upon him, would do such a thing. There's no way he would say such a thing.\" And the reason why they say this is because they have a specific image of him in their minds.", "Now, this image would be based on his life. It would be based on the characteristics and the qualities of the prophet, peace be upon him. It would be about his struggles in Mecca. It could be about his struggles in Medina. It could be about his mercy that he displayed upon attaining victory against Quraish. It could be his qualities and personality when it comes to dealing with orphans or the poor. It could be a bunch of things. Whatever it is, the Hadith rejector has an image in their mind of the prophet peace be upon him, and due to that image, they will say things like, \"There's no way he would say that Hadith.\"",), "Farid Responds, “The Psychology of Hadith Rejectors and the Solution”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Aelp49VAHtw", ((0, "0:00–1:06"), (68, "1:08–1:56"), (121, "2:01–2:44"), (165, "2:45–3:23"), (206, "3:26–4:02"),), id: 2032)
// U FR051:24-63 | Farid Responds | 3
#passage(("Hadiths are separate. Hadith A and hadith B, these are two hadiths, different subjects, from two different people. Why are you linking them? If one is unreliable, why does the other one automatically become unreliable? If you go through any book of history, you're going to find false information. You don't toss the book out the window. Herodotus in the Histories mention something about snakes with wings. You don't toss the book out of the window. And there is no historian that does that, because you expect to find false information in any book of history. So the hadiths should not be rejected because you find one or two or three or four questionable hadith.", "This same thought process, finding a problematic hadith and having an issue with it, is not something that you're discovering. It's something that the scholars of Islam have realized way before you. 1,400 years ago even the Sahabah themselves were cautious with a hadith that they came across, and then you had scholars that came after them that sifted through hadith, that filtered hadith, that critiqued hadith, that would accuse people of fabricating hadith. They did all of that. And notice how that's different from what you're doing. What you're doing is you're saying, \"Oh no, I got to reject everything.\" What, you say that the prophet peace be upon him ate chicken? That's a hadith. I reject hadith. Why would anyone even fabricate that in the first place? In any case, it's an unnatural reaction.", "And you got to be aware that this is a major issue, because there is no religion in which the followers of that religion reject all the information about their prophet. Imagine a Christian rejecting all the biographical information about Jesus. It's something that no one does except for the hadith rejectors. So this just goes to show you that this reaction is absolutely unnatural.",), "Farid Responds, “A Simple Solution for Quran Only / Hadith Rejectors”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=61ifU69I_II", ((163, "2:43–3:38"), (221, "3:41–4:34"), (282, "4:42–5:11"),), id: 2033)
