#passage(("My main argument, which I am yet to see a proper response to — and it's a really simple argument that can be used by anyone — the argument is that Hadith rejection cannot be accepted as a valid methodology or a valid Islamic sect because it's a very late development. There were no Hadith rejectors in the first century of Islam. There is not a single companion among the companions of the prophet, peace be upon him, that was a Hadith rejector. There were no books that were written by Hadith rejectors. There was not a single classical Hadith rejecting scholar.", "Now, what Hadith rejectors will say in response to this is, \"You Sunni's wrote history, and therefore you guys erased that part of history, the history of the Hadith rejectors,\" which does not make any sense, because Sunnis wrote about the Qadariyyah, the Shi'ah, the Mu'tazilah. We wrote about the sects that we have issues with. We know who their scholars are. We know what their books are, and their books are printed, and they're available for people. But you don't have a classical Hadith rejecting sect. You don't have classical Hadith rejecting books. You cannot name one single classical Hadith rejecting scholar.", "We even have books of heresiographies, which list out all the false sects. It will include all these minor Shi'i groups, all these minor Khawarij groups, how each of these groups split into different smaller sects, and the responses to these sects, and to how these sects fail, and you do not find a classical Hadith rejecting sect. That just doesn't exist.", "And to be honest, if Hadith rejection existed as a movement from the beginning, then you would find companions all being Hadith rejectors. But let's assume that the Hadith followers just popped up early on in Islam. You'd find a split. You'd find major difference of opinion. Now, on the other hand, even the political factions that went to war with each other, they all followed Hadiths.", "And after this point is expressed to a Hadith rejector, they need to sit down and think to themselves: are they truly following what the prophet, peace be upon him, taught? Because the first generations of Islam clearly seem to be oblivious to it. Or will the Hadith rejector turn a blind eye to this argument in order to follow their desires, because they really hate these few specific hadiths so much? So, a sincere hadith rejector would concede to this argument, since there's no way that the first generations of Islam got it wrong, and those that came after them by centuries and centuries and centuries got it right.",), "Farid Responds, “The Psychology of Hadith Rejectors and the Solution”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Aelp49VAHtw", ((767, "12:47–13:17"), (797, "13:17–14:03"), (843, "14:03–14:28"), (868, "14:28–14:57"), (900, "15:00–15:40"),), id: 2035)
