#passage(("The hadith rejector feels that everything in Islam is a conspiracy. The whole hadith thing is a conspiracy. There's not supposed to be any of these rules that are outside the Quran. That's a conspiracy. Where is this hadith rejection movement? What you'll have is one person saying, \"Hey, I don't agree with this hadith,\" but people rejecting every hadith outside the Quran didn't exist in the first century. And if it did exist, there'd be traces of it.",), "Farid Responds, “I had to delete my last video. [Quran-only]”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dA-hJ-ucpSA", ((264, "4:24–5:03"),), id: 2036)

=== 33.6 Rejecting all hadith because of some
// NODE P06.C01.S06
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR106:30-73 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("This idea of, \"Hey, there are Hadith that I just don't like, so I don't want to follow any Hadith\" — that's a strange way of approaching Hadith, and honestly approaching history. There are many matters in history that we find questionable, and it's not a reason to reject history as a whole. Imagine someone said, \"I don't believe that Brutus killed Caesar. I believe this is a fabrication, therefore I can't trust history.\" No one says that. The natural thing to do is to say, \"Yeah, that specific thing I don't trust.\" But for you to reject history as a whole, that's a strange jump. And unfortunately we find that with Hadith. You have perhaps a narration or two that you find problematic, and instead of saying, \"Yeah, those specific narrations I have trouble swallowing,\" you go from there to rejecting thousands of narrations. That doesn't really follow; that doesn't really make too much sense.", "And what's strange is, as opposed to history, Hadith, due to the care of the [classical] Muslim Scholars — they were so rigorous in terms of authenticating the reports, as opposed to any form of classical history, Roman, Greek, Persian. You won't find anything similar to Hadith in terms of rigorousness. So personally, what would make sense to me is to accept Hadith and reject all of history. That would make a lot more sense than to accept all of history and reject all of Hadith. That just doesn't make any sense to me.",), "Farid Responds, “Are Hadiths Necessary? Isn't the Quran Enough?”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6CA1zIFU8cM", ((82, "1:22–2:49"), (169, "2:49–3:34"),), id: 2037)
// U FR012:47-89 | Farid Responds | 6
#passage(("So, why is this outward Hadith rejection expressed in the first place? The reason is because they want to be consistent. They view it as a need to reject a Hadith. However, it's easier said than done. It's not something that you can do so easily when you've soaked your heart into Islam. In the back of their minds, there are these narrations that affect the way they view the religion. They affect the way they view the prophet peace be upon him. It's only when a Sunni prompts them with a Hadith, mentions that to them, they have to refresh their minds and say, \"Yes, that I reject.\"", "Now, here is the reality, something that Hadith rejectors don't really take into consideration, which is that Hadith rejectors have not studied Hadiths. There are certain Hadiths that have been brought to the attention of Hadith rejectors that they found problematic, but that's not the case with the vast majority of Hadiths. If a Hadith rejector sat down and read Hadiths, they would notice that it's only a very small number of narrations that they actually have a problem with. Those narrations that they do find problematic, they can be found in the sea of gems that are the Hadith collections: those gems that made them fall in love with the prophet peace be upon him in the first place, those same gems that caused them to attach themselves to the religion.", "However, what happens to them is what happens to you when you go to a restaurant for the first time and you try out a dish that you've never tried out before, and it's not good. You just don't like it. You go back home and you talk to your friends about it. You talk to family about it. They say, \"Oh, the restaurant's amazing.\" So, you try it out the next week, and then once again you choose a bad meal, and it's something that doesn't sit well with you, and you decide to never go to that restaurant again. If someone sat down and actually had all the meals in the restaurant, I'm sure they wouldn't feel that way, but they just got unlucky.", "And this one, when it comes to Hadith, it's a lot more sinister, because the narrations that people find problematic are ones that are, again, highlighted by opponents of Islam. But it's so rare for it to happen. If you opened up Sahih al-Bukhari and you just put your finger on a random Hadith, and you did it 20 times, the chances of you actually finding a Hadith that you actually find problematic is quite low.", "But again, the problematic Hadiths are the ones that are highlighted, warped, and misconstrued by opponents of Islam. And that's why they stand out. It's because they're brought to you. They're shoved in your face. And that's just the nature of the internet. It's nature of social media. A position that is not controversial is one that you barely come across. It's the controversial stuff that will show up in the top of your feed. And all of a sudden, you start assuming that all narrations are problematic, or a very large portion of narrations are problematic. That's not the case.", "So, the first thing that a Hadith rejector needs to be aware of is that Hadiths are not mainly controversial. Most of them are not controversial at all. And in fact, a lot of Hadiths are appealing to Hadith rejectors, which is why they have such a grand image, a grand view of the prophet, peace be upon him, in the first place.",), "Farid Responds, “The Psychology of Hadith Rejectors and the Solution”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Aelp49VAHtw", ((245, "4:05–4:43"), (283, "4:43–5:35"), (337, "5:37–6:17"), (377, "6:17–6:46"), (406, "6:46–7:21"), (441, "7:21–7:46"),), id: 2038)
// U FR012:122-146 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("So far I've been conceding to the usage of the term problematic, and what exactly is that about? In the eyes of a Hadith rejector, a Hadith is problematic because it conflicts with their values, conflicts with their morals, and it's something that they consider to be unfitting of God to legislate, or it would be unfitting for the messenger, peace be upon him, to act upon or to say. In most cases, Hadith rejectors bring these objections because they are themselves influenced by their environment, and they're influenced by the modern world. And that suggests that these problems are quite subjective. And I say this with certainty because you do find converts to Islam that were raised in very liberal backgrounds. However, they accept the Hadiths wholeheartedly as authentic and they find them as not problematic at all. And not only that, but if you brought five Hadith rejectors and put them in a room and you ask them questions to do with their morals and values, they would all disagree with one another. So that sort of thing is completely subjective.", "At this point, the Hadith rejector is saying, \"Yeah, but why should I care about other Hadith rejectors? I don't care about converts. I know that these reports are problematic because they're problematic to me.\" And this is actually the crux of the issue. We all have different standards. We all have different morals. We all have different values, or at least different priorities for each of these. And it's here that all Muslims need to do these two things. The first is that we need to be aware that our morals and values and priorities could be wrong unless they are dictated by God himself. And the second is that you need to be ready to submit to whatever Allah Subhana Wa Ta'ala has said. If others need to tweak their morals and values to fit in with the decree of Allah, with his religion, why should you be exempt from doing this? What makes you so special? You're not special. I'm not special. No one's special.",), "Farid Responds, “The Psychology of Hadith Rejectors and the Solution”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Aelp49VAHtw", ((641, "10:41–11:46"), (706, "11:46–12:45"),), id: 2039)
// U FR012:90-121 | Farid Responds | 4
#passage(("Another aspect of the issue is that Hadith rejectors have a problem with methodology. This is how the psychology works. They find a narration that is deemed authentic by Ahl al-Sunnah or a specific Sunni scholar. And then they think to themselves, \"Hey, if that's authentic, if that is how the system is run, then I cannot trust the system.\" And this assumption or this approach is very dangerous, because if you apply this to other fields, then you cannot trust in any field of science. Actually, it extends way past science. Imagine the jury decides unanimously that so-and-so is guilty of a crime, and then it turns out, after coming across additional evidence, that so-and-so is actually innocent. Oops, looks like we need to get rid of the jury system.", "Have forensic experts ever made the mistakes when it came to identifying fingerprints? Of course, they have. Are we going to toss away fingerprints as a valid method of crime scene investigation now? Of course, not. Have radiologists made mistakes when it came to reading scans? According to one study, which was a mix of normal cases and abnormal cases, radiologists had an error rate of 3.5 to 4%. I've never heard of anyone say that we need to get rid of radiology.", "That's one thing I don't understand about Hadith rejectors. They will be tolerant with other fields. They will grant them error rates. However, they won't do the same for scholars of Hadith. If you actually approached Hadith with that in mind, the possibility that a scholar could have made a mistake and yet the system in itself is still fine, then you'd be able to get past these few reports that you actually have an issue with.", "Of course, for the sake of clarity, I'll usually be siding with a Hadith scholar instead of the modern-day Hadith rejector when it comes to their differences of opinions in regards to a specific Hadith, but I'm just saying, from the perspective of a Hadith rejector: instead of dismissing the whole system, which has done a great job at catching additions, mistakes, fabrications, incorrect wordings, because of a few reports you don't like, why not just assume that there's an error rate and that some scholars of Hadith get some things wrong?",), "Farid Responds, “The Psychology of Hadith Rejectors and the Solution”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Aelp49VAHtw", ((466, "7:46–8:38"), (519, "8:39–9:15"), (556, "9:16–9:44"), (596, "9:56–10:41"),), id: 2040)
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML294:599-628 | The Muslim Lantern | 4
#passage(("Also a huge inconsistency that they have is the idea of accepting some Hadiths, and other Hadiths they don't accept, because they end up realising that they cannot know how to pray, they cannot know how to give Zakat (Obligatory Charity), they cannot know what is Ramadan, which month is Ramadan. They cannot explain anything in the Quran without using the Hadith. What they end up doing is that they end up accepting some Hadiths and rejecting other Hadiths. And they have a game that they play: they say that we accept the Hadith that are in line with the Quran. And what they mean by that is, we're accepting Hadiths that does not contradict the Quran, even if it adds new information; if this information does not contradict the Quran, we accept it.", "But the problem in this inconsistency is, if you accept that Hadith, then you're admitting that there can be reliable information passed down, because you're accepting that these Hadiths are OK, which means that we can pass down reliable information from the Prophetﷺ. Now what is the criteria for us to know what is being passed down is correct or not? If those individuals have passed down information, and you say that this information is correct because it's in line with the Quran, in the same time you're saying that they have passed down reliable information, which shows that they're able to pass down information reliably, which opposes what you said before, that they make mistakes. It's either they make mistakes, or they can pass down knowledge reliably because they pass down things which are in line with the Quran. You cannot play this game by picking and choosing what you like, and ignoring what you don't like.", "And that's why this idea of Hadith rejection is completely illogical, because it has to reject all history! The fact is that Islam did not start with those Hadith rejectors. It did not start 100 years before. This history of Hadith has started from the time of the Prophetﷺ. And if it started with the Prophetﷺ, then you have to completely reject the entire Islamic literature and history that has all of the information, because they believe scholars are traitors, they believe the people who wrote biographies are traitors. They believe everyone is a traitor and their understanding of the Quran is a correct understanding!", "Essentially, what they're saying is that you have to reject all Islamic history, all Islamic literature that has been written down, and all of those Muslims were fallible traitors that manipulated the religion, and no one has passed down the correct information except them today. And if this is not irrationality and illogical inconsistency, then I don't really know what is!",), "The Muslim Lantern, “A Complete Annihilation Of Hadith Rejector's Methodology! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1Gc0mbEqasg", ((2365, "39:25–39:52"), (2396, "39:56–40:36"), (2441, "40:41–41:05"), (2469, "41:09–41:21"),), id: 2041)
