#passage(("When it comes to Hadith rejectors and ex-Muslims in general, what they read is the few cherry-picked reports that are brought to them by opponents of Islam. That's what affects them. But when you actually read the Hadith and read the Quran for yourself from cover to cover, you see something completely different. You find something that captivates you, that makes you just curious about the way of life.",), "Farid Responds, “I just noticed something about these hadiths...”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXz5dQQbBVY", ((510, "8:30–8:59"),), id: 2042)
// U FR051:90-112 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("A hadith that is not weird — that's the vast majority of [Bukhari], Muslim. Have you ever sat down and read through the whole thing? You'd find that it'd be like 1% or less, the reports that you actually have an issue with. The vast majority of the reports you wouldn't have an issue with, and no thoughts about a motivation for fabrication would even cross your mind. One of my favorite hadith: Abu [Musa al-Ash'ari] himself saying the prophet peace be upon him ate chicken. The vast majority of hadiths are not even controversial in the first place. You'd read them and you'd be like, \"Oh, wow. Okay, that's interesting.\"", "And you have so much to gain from this. You gain proofs of prophethood. You gain information about the prophet sallallahu alaihi wasallam. You get to learn about the Quran itself. You get to learn about how Islam influenced that society, and how this group of people, the companions, they initially were pagan and now they all turned into Muslims, [monotheists]. You get to see the influence of that. You get to see the influence of the Sharia upon them. So, it's a wealth of information, and you're really missing out.",), "Farid Responds, “A Simple Solution for Quran Only / Hadith Rejectors”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=61ifU69I_II", ((472, "7:52–8:46"), (526, "8:46–9:19"),), id: 2043)

