#passage(("Is the hadith system a valid method of authenticating prophetic narrations? Ultimately, this is one of the main differences between the Sunnis and the Quran only community. I say this because if the prophet actually told someone \"this is haram\", then the average Quran only person would accept such a thing. So the main reason why, from my experience, from my discussions, Quran only folks don't accept these reports is because they don't believe that these reports are historically accurately representing what the prophet peace be upon him said.", "Those that are Quran only are missing out. You're missing out on the details of the life of the prophet peace be upon him. You're missing out on proofs of prophethood. The Quran praises the prophet peace be upon him, and sometimes that praise isn't as clear. If you know more about his life, you understand why the Quran praises him so much. And you're missing out on prohibitions and obligations. Now, I understand there are some things in the hadith that you feel uncomfortable with. However, the amount of stuff that you're missing out on outweighs the little discomfort that you find here and there with a hadith.",), "Farid Responds, “Debate: Farid vs MuhammadFromGod - Reliability of Hadith System”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Q3H7g7bsz3M", ((4, "0:04–1:02"), (71, "1:11–1:57"),), id: 2044)
// U FR051:1-23 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("There's so much to gain by accepting hadith. You learn about the prophet peace be upon him, you learn about his life, and you learn about his society, and through that you're able to learn about the Quran. You get to appreciate the Quran more. Your faith in Islam actually strengthens when you accept the hadith, because you actually realize the effect that the Quran had and Islam had on society as a whole.",), "Farid Responds, “A Simple Solution for Quran Only / Hadith Rejectors”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=61ifU69I_II", ((91, "1:31–2:01"),), id: 2045)
// U FR188:1-101 | Farid Responds | 5
#passage(("How do we know who Rasulullah was? Is that based on a hadith, or is it based on our gut feeling: \"I feel that he did this, I feel that he was like this\"? I remember this one guy from college. He said to me, \"Why do you have a beard?\" And I said, \"Well, what kind of question is that? Rasulullah had a beard.\" And he said, \"No, he didn't.\" And I said, \"Why do you assume that he didn't have a beard?\" And he said, \"Because I saw the prophet peace be upon him in a dream and he didn't have a beard.\"", "Is our knowledge of who Rasulullah was based on dreams? It's not based on dreams. It's not based on what we want to believe. It's based on who he was — that's in regards to his physical description, his words, his actions. Everything that makes him who he is is built on what we know of him from what's been reported by eyewitnesses, not by dreams, not by our gut feeling.", "And this is the issue with hadith rejecters: they will find things about Rasulullah that conflicts with their values. Think about it this way. We all have different values. We're all influenced by our environment, we're influenced by our own individual experiences, and based upon that we all as individuals view right and wrong differently. Morals and values differ based on our environments, our experiences. Now, with that in mind, what is acceptable from the actions of the prophet peace be upon him to me will not be acceptable to you, or vice versa, because of my experiences and my environment.", "However, when I see something that I know came from Rasulullah, I take a step back. I say to myself, \"[Farid], it doesn't matter how you feel, it doesn't matter what you think, because you are influenced by the media, you're influenced by the 21st century world, you're influenced by your friends. What you feel is right or wrong isn't necessarily so. And you take your morals, take them from Rasulullah, take them from what you find in the Quran and the authentic Sunnah.\"", "And that's the tragedy, and that's the predicament that hadith rejecters find themselves in. They've rejected hadith after hadith. It's the same with how Christians today view Jesus: it's not the historical Jesus, but it's unfortunately whatever they want him to be. Now we have Muslims doing the exact same thing. Imagine you revere a historical figure, you follow the religion that he came with, however you deny all the historical accounts that are attributed to him because some of that stuff conflicts with your morals and values. That's so sad. That's so pathetic.",), "Farid Responds, “Dear Hadith Rejecters”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k7zmZ_-evHk", ((5, "0:05–0:52"), (56, "0:56–1:42"), (102, "1:42–2:56"), (178, "2:58–3:40"), (222, "3:42–5:06"),), id: 2046)
// U FR002:92-124 | Farid Responds | 3
#passage(("We're not dealing with two competing texts. We're dealing with Quran and history. It's not Quran versus Hadith. It's Quran and history. Hadith is history. Hadith rejecters shouldn't have an issue with the history of the prophet, peace be upon him. Those that do, those that actually go out of their way, say, \"We cannot believe in the prophet, peace be upon him, eating chicken or eating dates or eating on a sufra\" — like one of those mats in which food is put upon — \"we cannot accept a Hadith in which the prophet, peace be upon him, is walking across town into the suq of Banu Qaynuqa'.\" Now, the reason why Hadith rejecters are that extreme, those of them that are at this level, is because they're attempting to be consistent, which is why they land in this absurd position.", "For example, if the prophet, peace be upon him, ordered stoning of those that fall under adultery, and that's found in Hadith, therefore the Hadith rejecters, instead of just rejecting that Hadith, they go all out and reject anything that's referred to as a Hadith, including the prophet, peace be upon him, praising members of the Ansar or tribes from among the Ansar, or the eating habits of the Ansar, or the eating habits of the Muhajireen, or what they used to ride when they used to go to the souk, or narrations that have to do with agriculture. This sort of reaction, it's a reaction to Hadith, nothing more. It's what causes them to reject everything, even matters that are not controversial at all. So this is what causes them to go way beyond just rulings. Instead of just rejecting that which is identified with as Hadith, they reject all of Islamic history.", "And it should be very obvious: one who rejects everything in the history of a certain civilization, especially one that has to do with the context of a holy book, will not have the understanding of someone that actually does accept history and the context of that same holy book. And that's why I have to be blunt. That is why Hadith rejecters are usually the most ignorant group that falls under the umbrella of Islam, even if we're going to grant them that. And on the other hand, those that immerse themselves in Hadith as well as the Quran, they're going to be the ones that understand the Quran the best.",), "Farid Responds, “Quran VS Hadith”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9eBzpof1AQE", ((489, "8:09–9:06"), (546, "9:06–10:22"), (622, "10:22–11:09"),), id: 2047)
