== 34. The reliability and methodology of hadith

=== 34.1 Isnad, matn and the conditions of authenticity
// NODE P06.C02.S01
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML127:556-586 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("For every Hadith, we will have it in numbers, because every book of Hadith will have chapters and numbers of Ahadith. Now, we have different books: we have Bukhari and Muslim, which are the overwhelming majority of correct Narrations, meaning they're all Authentic Narrations. Then we have other books, which can contain valid Narrations and Narrations which are weak. For example, we have anNasa'i, we have atTirmidhi, we have ibnMaja. We have all of these other books that have Narrations.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Researcher Confronts Muslim On Extremist Teachings! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=G5Mgp9SzUc0", ((1524, "25:24–25:49"),), id: 2049)
// U ML294:673-750 | The Muslim Lantern | 11
#passage(("What is a Hadith? A Hadith is that which was transmitted from the Prophetﷺ, from a statement, so something that he said, or an action, a specific action that he did, or something that he approved of. So something happened in the presence of the Prophetﷺ and he was OK with it; he didn't say to the people \"don't do\". Or the description of the Prophetﷺ, whether in his character, his manners, or how he was created, like his arms, his head. So anything of the statements, the sayings, the actions, the approvals, all of these things are what we call a Hadith. That's the definition of what a Hadith is.", "And the Hadith is divided into 2 parts. It is divided into what we call إسناد (Isnad), which is a Chain of Narration: the individuals who narrated this information, who gave us this information. So we say Alex narrated from Mark, narrated from John, that he heard the Prophetﷺ say this. So Alex and Mark and John, they are the Isnad, the Chain of Narration. And that the Prophetﷺ said \"1 plus 1 equals 2\", for example, hypothetically: this statement, \"1 plus 1 equals 2\", the actual statement that the Prophetﷺ said, we consider that the متن (Matn). The Matn is the actual wording that the Prophetﷺ said, and the Isnad or the سَنَد (Sanad) is the Chain of Narration, the individuals who pass down this information.", "And now all of these Ahadith, the statements and actions of the Prophetﷺ, are generally divided into 2 main categories: Authentic and Weak. Now there's a lot more different categories, but we'll just stick with those 2 main categories. What are these Authentic Ahadith? The Authentic Hadith is that which is transmitted from the Prophetﷺ by a person who is trustworthy, a person who is known in his community to be a trustworthy individual, who is a good individual, and he's a practicing Muslim, and he’s a strong memoriser: عدل ضابط. The 1st condition is that he is a trustworthy individual; 2nd is that his memorisation and memory is very strong. If it becomes weaker, then he's no longer fitting that criteria. So whoever he narrates from has to also have these 2 conditions: has to be strong in memory and has to be a good individual, known in the community to be a good individual.", "And the Chain has to be Connected: there cannot be any people missing in the Chain. You cannot narrate from someone that was not living at your time, that was living 200 years before you, because you cannot meet him or you cannot take information from him. So it has to be possible for you to meet that individual. Not only that, but also the Chain has to go all the way back to the Prophet. If the information reaches a Companion of the Prophet only, but it does not reach the Prophetﷺ, and he doesn't say \"the Prophet said\", then that is a statement of a Companion; it is not a Hadith of the Prophetﷺ. If it reaches a student of a Companion, then it is not a Narration of the Prophetﷺ; rather, it is a statement of a follower of a Companion of the Prophetﷺ.", "This is what Authentic Hadith is: that which is narrated by a trustworthy, just individual, well memoriser, a strong memory, who narrates from someone like him, tracing all the way back to the Prophetﷺ. And it cannot be irregular; that Narration cannot have any defects.", "Now, defects and irregularity, these are big things; there's a whole Science written on that. But I'll give a very basic example of شذوذ (Shudhudh: irregularity), which is, for example, where you have one person who told the story, and then there was six people sitting down. Five of those people narrated the story in a way, and one individual narrated the same story, but he added extra information that the other five did not say. That would mean that this extra part that was added is either a mistake or irregularity: the Narrator has made a mistake in attributing it to that Narrator when he did not narrate it.", "So we know that through tracing all of those different Chains of Narrations: we look at those people who narrated the Hadith, and we see if the majority are narrating one thing, and just one person who had an extra statement that the other people did not narrate, even though they've all heard the story from the same person. Then we say the extra statement that was made is irregular, and that statement can be rejected.", "Also the idea of hidden defects: there are defects that can remove the reliability of a Hadith, and there are defects that don't remove the reliability of the Hadith; the Hadith can be Authentic even though there's a hidden defect. For instance, if we say that the Narrator says \"I heard from Ibn 'Umar\", which is a Companion of the Prophet, and he actually heard it from Abu Hurayra, so both of those two different people are reliable, but the Narrator, there's a hidden defect there: he made a mistake and he heard it from a wrong person. He did not hear it from 'Abdullah Ibn 'Umar, he actually heard it from Abu Hurayra, but he mixed the two individuals. So that's a defect, but a hidden defect.", "Another hidden defect, for example, is someone narrates information, he attributes it to the Prophet, while actually it was not from the Prophetﷺ, but it was a story that was heard from the people of the Scriptures. That would be considered a hidden defect, and it will affect the Hadith. Now the Hadith is no longer an Authentic Narration that you have to believe in; rather, it's information taken from the people of the Scriptures.", "Those people who are rejecting Hadith have no clue of how much work the people have put to authenticate these Narrations, and for us to come to this conclusion: these Narrations have to be historically reliable. So it's not just like we found the letter and the king was signing it; but we know who the king is, we have the full biography of the king, we know who wrote the letter, which handler of the king wrote the letter, we know his student who took the letter from him, and we know his son and his son and his son and his friend, and whoever narrated that information until it reaches today, seeing it in front of our own eyes.", "This is our criteria, not the criteria of the weak Western history; but our criteria is even further and much stricter for us to accept anything coming from the Prophetﷺ or not coming from the Prophetﷺ, and how precise were those people actually in passing down the information.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “A Complete Annihilation Of Hadith Rejector's Methodology! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1Gc0mbEqasg", ((2648, "44:08–44:39"), (2679, "44:39–45:14"), (2721, "45:21–46:08"), (2774, "46:14–46:52"), (2816, "46:56–47:07"), (2834, "47:14–47:43"), (2869, "47:49–48:04"), (2889, "48:09–48:38"), (2921, "48:41–48:57"), (2940, "49:00–49:34"), (2978, "49:38–49:49"),), id: 2050)
// U ML278:198-262 | The Muslim Lantern | 4
#passage(("I'm not saying the Hadith is a biography of the Prophet. I'm saying the Hadith is what the Prophet taught, what he was doing in his life after he received Messengerhood from God. So it's not a biography since he was born and after he died and all of that. No, there are books about the biography of the Prophet. It has more to do with the teachings and the things that he said to do with Islam, the Revelation from God. This is what the Hadith is.", "So he would give us Revelation. He will say that God commanded him to do this, or we should do this as a command of God, and is separate from the Quran. The Quran is the VERBATIM Word of Allah, while the Hadith is the teachings of the Prophet through Revelation. So, this is a separation between the two. We don't believe anyone speaks in the Quran other than Allah. It's all Allah's words. It's all Divine, verbatim, the Words of the Creator.", "So, when you say something in the Quran, then you have to have a chapter and a verse, and then you quote which verse, and then the verse has words. So, you actually read what the verse says. Now, when it comes to the Hadith, then you look at the Hadith: where does the Hadith exist? Because there are multiple books of Hadith. And then there's a criteria that we have. And all of this work is already done in books, and the books show you which is an authentic report and which is not, and why it is authentic report, and why it's not authentic report. So, now you can not just go on a Discord server, and then someone just types something, and then you copy-paste it and you go and say Islam says this. You can not be doing this.", "We follow everything authentic reported to the Prophet, meaning everything that we call صحيح, which means \"Authentic\", because there are different gradings of Hadith. This is not just something that we woke up one night and decided to do. This is a whole history of 1400 years of literature. It's a whole science called \"the Science of Hadith\". And within the Science of Hadith you have \"the Science of Men\", which is grading these different Hadith based on seeing these men, whether they're reliable or not. And then we have a Science of Grading: this Hadith is weak, this Hadith is authentic, this Hadith is fabricated. We have different grades: صحيح, موضوع, موقوف, مرفوع. We have many different gradings. It's a Science that people spend their life studying.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Family Grills Muslim On His Beliefs! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TKqmYTyocyo", ((648, "10:48–11:04"), (670, "11:10–11:24"), (688, "11:28–12:12"), (759, "12:39–13:15"),), id: 2051)
