#passage(("The first thing that I want you to be aware of, this is my first main argument, is that when it comes to the hadith system, there are two main conditions for determining the authenticity of a hadith. Those two conditions are the connectivity and the trustworthiness of the reports. Now what I want to get across to you is that this is not an exclusively Muslim thing. Since classical times you had Greek and Roman historians; when they would speak about [historiography], they would mention these conditions as conditions for authenticity, emphasizing connectivity, trustworthiness, long before Muslims did, hundreds of years before Muslims did.", "One of them is [Marcellinus]. He was the last great pagan historian. He says: \"As far as I have been able to investigate the truth, we have narrated those matters which we were able to witness in our life or to know of by carefully examining those who were centrally engaged.\" He passed away slightly before the 4th century. Here, what he's saying is we need eyewitnesses.", "And you have similar statements like this from classical historians in the 16th and 17th centuries throughout Europe. What they used to do was they had these [historiography] manuals that teach you how to approach history, and these were penned, again, by scholars in Europe, and they had the same idea. One of these scholars is a French historian and theologian called Honore [de Sainte-Marie], and he passed away in the year 1713. He said: \"As to well-known and not incredible events, one should accept without question the testimony of those writers under whose eyes they have happened, or who have learned of them from honest, trustworthy contemporaries living in the country where the events occurred.\" A contemporary is a person living in the same age. \"As [to] events which are not public, but which contain nothing fabulous, one should regard them as probable when they occur in the country of the writer, and highly probable when recounted by eyewitnesses or by contemporaries who do not seem to wish to deceive.\"", "And one more: according to George Cornwall Lewis, the author of Inquiry to the Credibility of Early Roman History, \"unless these witnesses had personal and immediate perception of the facts which they report, unless they saw and heard what they undertake to relate as having happened, their evidence is not entitled to credit. As all original witnesses must be contemporary with the events which they attest, it is a necessary condition for the credibility of a witness that he be a contemporary, though a contemporary is not necessarily a credible witness.\"", "So why am I saying this? It's because that's what Muslim Sunni scholars of hadith said: you got to mention who you're quoting. When they start quoting the person they're narrating from, then it's accepted, because now it's connected. When they don't quote their person, their teacher, it's something that is not accepted by early hadith scholars. Now the scholars of hadith not only paid attention to individual cases, but they actually wrote books on the subject: there are books listing out the people that heard and the people that did not hear, just so that you know that you're getting a connected report.", "Now remember there are two conditions. The other condition is the reliability of the eyewitnesses: maybe he was there, but maybe he's not trustworthy. Even before the common era, [Polybius], the Greek historian, says something similar. He says: \"Whenever one or two falsehoods are discovered in treatises, and this is deliberate, then it is clear that nothing said by such authors is reliable or certain.\" And by the way, notice he says one or two. Muslims did not do one or two. If you're a Muslim, you did it once, you're gone. You're not accepted as a hadith narrator; you're rejected as a liar. Mubarak says: \"The punishment of a liar is that his truth is rejected.\" [Imam Ahmad] said: \"His repentance is between him and Allah, but his hadith should never be written again.\"", "This is universal criteria. It's not criteria they made up on their own. It's something that all historians agree to. You have different civilizations; they all came to these conclusions: trustworthiness, connectivity.", "Let me just give you an example. This is one book. He shows you his work: he lists out the narrators, he quotes their hadiths, and he shows you, based on the narrations and based on what they're quoting, whether they're reliable or not. It's not some sort of game. It's not some sort of guesswork. This is an actual study in actual science. And by the way, this is one of the books; there's tens of books like this.", "Now the scholars, they knew that just because some hadiths are weak, it doesn't mean that all hadiths are weak. Just because some liars existed doesn't mean that every narrator is a liar. Just because some hadiths were disconnected, it doesn't mean that every hadith was disconnected. If a narrator is established to be reliable, if the chain is considered to be connected, then the report is by default considered reliable. Is this criteria sufficient to determine the reliability of a report? If not, then what else is needed?",), "Farid Responds, “Debate: Farid vs MuhammadFromGod - Reliability of Hadith System”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Q3H7g7bsz3M", ((228, "3:48–4:38"), (278, "4:38–5:04"), (304, "5:04–6:14"), (374, "6:14–6:50"), (413, "6:53–8:25"), (505, "8:25–9:30"), (583, "9:43–10:03"), (603, "10:03–10:36"), (641, "10:41–11:46"),), id: 2052)
