#passage(("If you're looking at a Hadith, there's a grading system for them. So a Hadith has different gradings: authentic and Weak. And then for Authentic, you have different gradings. For instance, the highest grading is مُتَواتِر (Mutawatir: which is mass transmitted). So it's so massively transmitted by so many people in so many different locations that it's impossible for anyone to fabricate this. Cannot happen. People who haven't met each other, they all are reporting the same thing, tracing to the same source, which shows that this is the highest level, now called Mutawatir Hadith. And then you have Authentic Hadith. And then that Authentic will also have different gradings, like the number of people in the Chain, how many people report the same report, etc. But authentic is all accepted as a Revelation, because \"it's authentic\" means it's authentically going back to the Prophet.", "And then you have a Weak Hadith, and that also has different gradings. So for instance, you can have a Hadith where you have a liar in the Chain, and then that Hadith would be weak because there is a liar, so we'd never accept this Hadith. You can have a Hadith which is Weak, but just the person in the Chain became weak in memory when he got old, even though he was very strong in memory when he was younger. Even that is recorded in the Books of Hadiths: WE RECORD EVERYTHING. So we would say this Hadith is weak because it's possible that now this person has become older in age, and his memory is weak, that he made a mistake.", "But that is not as weak as a liar in the Chain, because liar in the Chain means: we don't trust this! But this guy is a trustworthy person, but when he became older, he became a little bit weaker. So it's Weak, but it's not at the same level of weakness. Then you have very very weak, which is Fabricated: someone just completely made up a Narration. And we can know that by tracing the sources. So that would be completely REJECTED. It's not allowed for anyone to narrate this or follow it. So there's levels of Authentic and there's levels of Weak.", "What is an authentic Narration? It has 5 conditions. Authentic Hadith is: 1- ما رَواهُ (what is narrated) عَدْلٌ ضابِطٌ. And عدل means someone trustworthy, meaning his character in his community: he's known to be a righteous man, he's not a liar, he's not a thief, he's not an evil person, he doesn't do immoral actions, he's known of piety in his community. So that is one condition: عدالة–عدل, these piety and truthfulness. It's justice.", "2- ضابِطٌ. And ضابِطٌ means that he is strong in memory. And also there are scholars who would narrate from their writing. So some scholars will write, they will have a كتاب (a book), they have the Narrations written, and they will narrate from there. So some scholars, for example, in grading will say he's strong if he narrates from his book, but if he's narrating from his memory, then this hadith is weak. So we're very, very precise.", "So ما رَواهُ عَدْلٌ ضابِطٌ عَن مِثْلِهِ: so now he needs to narrate from someone like him as well. 3- بِسَنَدٍ مُتَّصِل (With a connected Chain). So now the Chain of Narration needs to connect all the way back to the Prophet. I need to have the possibility of narrating from that person. And Bukhari even takes that a step further, proving that they met each other, and all of that: met each other, took from each other, lived at the same time, and been in the same place. So he takes it to even a higher level. That's why it's the most Authentic book. You cannot possibly narrate from someone in a city or a country you've never been to, for instance. So this will be called a \"Disconnected Chain\". Then, 4-5: مِنْ غَيْرِ شُذوذٍ وَلا عِلّة. Now there is 2 defects that can exist in the Chain: 4- شُذوذ (irregularity) and 5- علّة (hidden defect).",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Researcher Demands Evidence Against The Crucifixion Of Jesus! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IVUeaRBg02o", ((465, "7:45–8:45"), (530, "8:50–9:24"), (570, "9:30–10:00"), (602, "10:02–10:36"), (643, "10:43–11:25"), (697, "11:37–13:01"),), id: 2053)
