#passage(("Every Hadith, even weak Ahadith, you would find that they would have something called a chain of narration, meaning they will have the individuals that heard that information going back to the source. Or they would have people who heard the information, that they have a link, but maybe the link is missing one person, or maybe the link has a specific issue. Do you find any chains of transmission for the Bible? Do you find any group of people that we can trace back all the way to Jesus, knowing that this information came from him?", "By the way, I'm talking about weak Hadith, not even authentic Hadith. So I would say even weak Hadith are even stronger historically than the Bible, because, first, they're closer to the source: the written form, they're probably closer to the source. Number two, they have individuals that we can trace all the way back to the source.", "In that chain of narration every individual in the chain has to be a trustworthy honest person. In the past, people were actual communities; there was no social media; people used to know the neighbors. They used to know their very far neighbours; even if someone does something bad, the whole Community knows about it. So people were actually communities, not like today, people who separate from one another. If someone was a liar, he's known in the community as a liar. If someone is a trustworthy individual, people already know this person is a trustworthy individual. This was how communities were: if I knew you lied, now the whole Community will know that you are a liar.", "And there was very strict criteria there, like, for example, if someone was to even be evil to the animals, like play games with an animal, try to put food in front of the animal and when the animal comes he hides the food: that was enough for people not to accept Hadith from that individual. It used to be very intricate when it comes to these issues, because it is religion for us.", "Secondly, they need to have a good memory, strong memory. And what happened, they used to test the people to see their memory. So an individual, he narrates from another individual all the way going to the Prophet; other people used to come, and then they used to mix the chain of narration and ask questions, to see whether that person still has a good memory: he will know this is his own Hadith or someone else's Hadith. If he says, \"No, this is not from me, this is wrong, this is incorrect,\" then they know that person still has a correct strong memory. People used to rely on their memory, no pads; people had to rely on their memory, remember your address, remember the way of where to go, where to come. So memories were stronger, because the muscle was exercised more than it is today. And Arabs used to rely on memory. This was their thing.", "The point is: is there anything even remotely close to that regarded in the Bible?",), "The Muslim Lantern, “American Lady Questions Muslim On Hadith Reliability! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dWwbHO5Owpc", ((120, "2:00–2:37"), (166, "2:46–2:59"), (203, "3:23–3:55"), (246, "4:06–4:19"), (263, "4:23–5:15"), (382, "6:22"),), id: 2054)
