#passage(("So your memory doesn't have to be perfect. Everyone makes mistakes; there's not a person alive who's perfect in memory, and therefore you don't have to have a perfect memory.", "\"Be familiar with the contents of his notebooks and be able to recognize the slightest changes which people intentionally or inadvertently may introduce to them\": it's not referring to a transmitter being [familiar] with five different wordings. The narrator must be familiar with his own books, and if someone introduces a change to his book, then the person should be able to catch it. That's what it's saying.", "While there may have been one example of someone saying this guy was urinating while standing, or this guy was tugging his beard or whatever, that's not how narrators are judged. There are generally two main things that lead to a narrator being rejected: memory and trustworthiness. That's it. Those are the main two things. It's not like you have a major narrator of Hadith and one day someone catches him tugging his beard, or feeding his chicken, or playing with doves, or playing with his dog, and they're like, okay, we can't take from him. If you have an example of that, that's like a one off, and at the same time, if anyone does that, the vast majority of Scholars are not going to drop him for that. It's not a reason to drop someone.", "When it comes to gaps, that's a reason to reject a Hadith. Gaps are not an issue with [narrator] himself. If you're [narrating] with gaps, no one's going to say you're weak; they're just going to reject that specific Hadith.",), "Farid Responds, “Free Education for David Wood (Condensed to 2.5h)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hBH4MpJeYIw", ((192, "3:12–3:36"), (249, "4:09–4:51"), (433, "7:13–8:44"), (524, "8:44–9:01"),), id: 2055)
