#passage(("So let's say someone's finally been able to provide you with a primary source. Now it's important to look into the topic of authenticity, because if the report is not authentic, then there's no reason for you to rely upon it; it's not binding upon you. And there's something to keep in mind: there are many reasons as to why our scholars preserve information.", "Sometimes authors preserve information in order to be comprehensive; it's not something that's exclusive to Islamic books, it's something that you can find in all sorts of works of history. Sometimes authors provide conflicting information in order to provide you with multiple points of view. Sometimes authors will provide you with fabricating reports because they want you to be aware of what has been fabricated. Sometimes authors will provide you with weak reports in order for you to be aware of the weakness of certain narrators. So the first thing that you need to be aware of is the intention of the author from writing the book itself, before you're able to consume the information that you find within the book.", "So the first tip is to look into who the main narrator of the hadith is. If it's a late narrator, if it's someone that never met the prophet, peace be upon him, then that someone is not someone that you can rely upon. Only connected reports are binding upon you; disconnected reports aren't. Another simple tip is to go to websites that provide you with a preliminary grading of the report, something like [sunnah.com]. Usually at the bottom of the hadith you will find the grading that will give you an idea as to whether the hadith is authentic or not. Of course, the gradings on these websites will never substitute actually learning [ilm al-hadith]; however, if you don't know Arabic, then it is a very useful tool, and it will help you out.", "Sometimes there are some clear signs that the hadith is not authentic, and what you can simply do is you can ask the person that's using the report against you if the hadith is authentic or not. And some of them, they've actually done the research, and they actually know that it's a weak report, but they still want to use it against you anyhow. So what you need to do is you need to push them into a corner by simply asking them: are you sure that this report is reliable?", "Now another tip that comes to mind is relying on the footnotes, because they save you a lot of time and a lot of research in many cases, especially with Arabic books, especially if it's an academic print. You will find the editors including footnotes at the bottom of a hadith that will allow you to arrive at a grading. Depending on the book, of course: sometimes it's a commercial print; commercial prints usually don't include hadith gradings; however, academic prints do. And some people unfortunately have no idea how to deal with footnotes.",), "Farid Responds, “Refutations 101 [Crash Course]”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CgfiwsD4Sns", ((695, "11:35–11:57"), (719, "11:59–12:44"), (777, "12:57–13:43"), (823, "13:43–14:11"), (866, "14:26–14:58"),), id: 2059)
// U FR237:47-79 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("Alhamdulillah, in Islam we don't need fabrications; we don't use fabrications. And just because it's something that I would like doesn't make it authentic. And the same applies to weakening authentic hadiths that conflict with our social norms. Are we going to weaken those? No, we don't. And the people that do are either extremely ignorant [in] hadith sciences, or they're just simply [insincere]. So this has nothing to do with our subjectivity and our desires, nor does it have anything to do with our feelings and our emotions. We have a science of hadith, and we weaken and authenticate reports based on that science.",), "Farid Responds, “I Refuse to Accept this Miracle for Islam!!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Nw9Tl8RwWLM", ((120, "2:00–2:52"),), id: 2060)
