#passage(("However, we don't use the word scholar lightly. There are many, many hadith all scholars agree upon. For example, [Bukhari]: all scholars agree this is the most authentic hadith book. Then Muslim is the next one. This is what scholars agree on. However, there are some narrations that you can have scholars disagreeing over [its] authenticity. And this is the minority of the narrations, where you have disagreements.", "When I say scholars, I'm not talking about today. We're talking about traditional scholars, scholars who are the most knowledgeable, scholars who are close to that time, scholars who knew the people in the chain and stuff like that. These scholars, if they disagree over a narration, then yes, you could follow the scholar that weakens the narration. So it doesn't have to be abiding for you, because if it's weak, that means the prophet haven't said it. If the prophet didn't say it, then it's not abiding for you, because it's not revelation. What makes it binding is it's authentically attributed to the prophet.", "However, you have even major scholars close to our day and age as well. I'm not completely denying every scholar in our day age. However, I'm saying everything is based on the evidence, because weakening the narrations is also based on the evidence. So, for example, someone calls himself a scholar today and he says to you this narration is weak, but all the classical scholars agree that it's authentic: we don't care what he says. This is not a valid disagreement. However, if a scholar today says this [narration] is weak, and he cites earlier scholars saying it's weak, and another scholar says it's authentic, and he cites earlier scholars saying it's authentic, the people in the chain, then you have a case.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Confident Agnostic Confronts A Muslim About The Treatment Of Jews In Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IzncpPt4QVo", ((1086, "18:06–18:30"), (1115, "18:35–19:14"), (1204, "20:04–20:46")