// U FR260:1-181 | Farid Responds | 7
#passage(("One of the major [tabi'in] that lived in Basra said that when we heard narrations, we would not stop until we went to Medina and we heard directly from the sahaba.", "Another really interesting [athar]. Have you heard of [Ayla]? Today it's around the southern tip of Israel, modern day. He says that in the year 150 he went to Makkah to meet Ibn Juraj. Ibn Juraj was technically the mufti of Makka at the time. He gets to [Makkah]; [Ibn Juraj] passes away. Now Khalid bin Nizar says that he had with him the books of Ibn Juraj, and what he had to do was he had to read those books upon the students of [Ibn Juraj].", "How did Khaled bin [Nizar] come to have the books in the first place? What they used to do is they used to go to copyists, and they would say: can you copy this book from me? And the copyist would spend a while writing it down, and then he would sell it to him. Another possibility is he heard the books from one of the [mashayikh] in [Ayla] and he then wrote everything down. But the key thing is he has the narrations, but he still wants to make the trip to Makkah to hear it directly. That's extreme, making sure and double checking, but that's how it was, and that's how they used to take knowledge.", "Some of my favorite examples similar to this is a famous [athar] about [Shu'bah ibn al-Hajjaj]. He was the leader of the believers in hadith of his time. He himself was a school of hadith, to be honest, and from him came major students. [Shu'bah] was really famous for this, for being extremely critical when it came to hadiths.", "So he narrates that he heard this narration: that he and some of the sahaba used to take turns and take shifts when it came to taking care of the camels, so he was somewhat late to come to the [majlis] of rasulallah. He comes in and he hears: whoever makes a good [wudu], [prays two] raka's and asks for forgiveness, then he would be forgiven. And he is astounded by this hadith, and he reacts in a positive way, and then someone pulls him from behind and he says: you missed something that was a lot better than this before you came. So he turns around and he finds [Umar]. So Omar says: you missed when he said that whoever testifies that there is no god but Allah and that the prophet is his messenger, then he shall enter whichever of the doors of heaven he pleases.", "So he goes looking for Abdullah, and according to one narration (just for context purposes, he is in Basra), he travels to Makkah to hear this narration. He gets to [Makkah] and he asks: I heard this narration; it's supposed to be you narrating it from a famous sahabi. He was the emir of Egypt for three years during the time [of Mu'awiya], so major sahabi. So that's according to one report. According to another report, he says: I heard this from Ibrahim Abdul Rahman bin Auf, who is also in Medina. So [Shu'bah] heads off to Medina; it's not too far now, at least it's a bit closer. He gets to Medina and he meets [Sa'd] Ibrahim. He asks him: did you hear this hadith? \"I didn't hear this.\" So he has to go back to Iraq. So he gets back to Iraq, and he says: did you narrate this narration? And he says: no, I didn't narrate this. And it was with that that [Shu'bah] gave up on this hadith.", "Now there's something really interesting about this narration: parts of it, the first part, about praying, that narration can be found in Sahih Muslim. So to [Shu'bah], it's not a matter of: is this hadith authentic or weak? These guys, they were so obsessed with knowledge that they took themselves to the next level in order to find out: is this true, is this not true, did he narrate it like this?",), "Farid Responds, “The Quest for the Hadith”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PilW7FdMHa0", ((2, "0:02–0:13"), (25, "0:25–1:25"), (99, "1:39–2:28"), (150, "2:30–3:26"), (207, "3:27–4:39"), (296, "4:56–6:59"), (422, "7:02–7:56"),), id: 2072)
// U FR047:242-301 | Farid Responds | 3
#passage(("This applies to all these rulings that the people were witnessing the prophet peace be upon him performing and doing and living by, and that's why we have all these reports. You have all of these Muslims that are doing things in salah. For example, they would all raise their hands when they start off the prayer. This is preserved. The scholars preserve these opinions. At the same time they also preserve the differences. When you make a mistake in salah, do you do your prostrations after or you do them before? Do you do both sides or you do it one side? So you see, it's not a conspiracy, because they preserved the agreements and they preserved the disagreements.", "And by the way, this isn't just a point for the compilers of Sunni hadith. It's a point for the compilers of Shia hadith as well, because the Shia scholars, they would preserve the different opinions that have been attributed to their imams, which is proof to me that, for example, [Kulayni] isn't the guy who created 16,000 hadith. You have classical narrators that fabricated, sure. But I'm not going to accuse the compiler of fabricating all these differences. Did he fabricate some? Sure. I actually do accuse him of tampering with some reports. But is he the cause for all of these reports? No, because he's including contradicting reports.", "It's something that the community itself as a whole is preserving. In general, you do have sometimes the community following a specific thing, because that's how it's being transferred. They cannot all agree to lie.",), "Farid Responds, “I had to delete my last video. [Quran-only]”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dA-hJ-ucpSA", ((904, "15:04–16:06"), (970, "16:10–16:59"), (1077, "17:57–18:22"),), id: 2073)
