#passage(("What we're going to do is we're going to be testing the system. We need to test the system; we need to see if this works.", "If everything usually comes from [Bukhari] and Co, how do we know about him, if all we know about him is from himself? It's not from him. The information that we know about [Bukhari] is not from him; it's from his peers.",), "Farid Responds, “Refuting the Claim: Hadiths were Fabricated 250 Years Later”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Q-h8L0-qS4g", ((4, "0:04–0:45"), (61, "1:01–1:13"),), id: 2075)
// U FR112:121-188 | Farid Responds | 3
#passage(("The third criticism: the authors of Hadith works are the ones who fabricated hadiths, and hadiths are 250 years old, 300 years old, whatever. This is quite an archaic criticism, and critics of Islam, you need to be aware that intellectual Western academics have moved on from this because of how bad this argument is.", "The compilers, the ones that passed away in the year 250 to the year 300, they're not the sole sources for these hadiths. You'll find, for example, [Bukhari] and Muslim and Abu [Dawud] all narating a single Hadith. They're narrating the same thing, and they're not narrating it from one another; they're narrating it from different people. Those different people would be narating it from other different people, and usually what happens is those others are narrating it from a common source, and that common source would usually be someone from the first century or someone from the early 2 Century, and of course they're quite early. So folks in Western Academia have pushed hadiths forward instead of pushing them back, and started to accuse these early narrators of fabricating these reports, instead of this view that the compilers of hadiths fabricated these reports, because the data conflicts with this.", "The idea that [Bukhari] fabricate Hadith or Muslim fabricate Hadith: the only reason why arguments like these are popular is because folks in my comments watch garbage on YouTube. The folks that are popular are not folks that are educated in Islam or in criticisms of Islam, and due to that they use these arguments that have been rejected by intellectual critics of Islam.",), "Farid Responds, “Three Common Claims about Hadith”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=otvYP10qsDo", ((309, "5:09–5:50"), (354, "5:54–7:20"), (443, "7:23–8:01"),), id: 2076)
// U FR112:79-120 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("Second question is usually something along the lines of: you Muslims authenticate Hadith that you like and reject hadiths that you dislike. I don't even know, when people say this, are they really being sincere? Because any critic of Islam, I'm sure they're aware that they're quoting things from [Bukhari] and Muslim when criticizing Islam, and they know that these narrations are authentic according to Muslims, and that Muslims accept these reports that are being brought forward to them. The average educated Muslim will be like: yeah, this is authentic; however, I have an issue with your understanding of the Hadith. Usually the Muslim will not reject the Hadith.", "And also vice versa: we have narrations that are like merits for the prophet peace be upon him, miracles of the Prophet peace be upon them, that Muslims themselves reject. I'll give you one example: the prophet peace be upon him and Abu Bakr were escaping [Mecca], and they were surrounded by the polytheists in the cave, and what happened then was some spiders came and they built a web around the cave. This narration, it's quite a famous popular narration; however, it does not meet the standards of an authentic Hadith, which is why you have some classical scholar that have simply said: yeah, this is a rejected report; this specific Hadith is weak. So even though it's something that would be used to strengthen Islam, it's something that we reject.",), "Farid Responds, “Three Common Claims about Hadith”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=otvYP10qsDo", ((206, "3:26–4:14"), (254, "4:14–5:03"),), id: 2077)
// U FR091:50-252 | Farid Responds | 5
#passage(("This is Hadith number 666: on very cold and rainy nights, Allah's messenger used to order to say: pray in your homes. In the Arabic it says Abdullah [bin Yusuf] from Malik. It's ironic that the first Hadith that's chosen is a Golden Chain Hadith. What are the odds?", "This Hadith is narrated by a bunch of people. This is from Malik, Malik of course quoting from [Nafi']. Abu daood, he's quoting from Malik. So it's a repetition in that sense: Muslim from [Yahya bin Yahya] from Malik. And then you have it; this is a bit different; it's the same meaning: during a cold and windy night the prophet peace be upon him said to pray in their dwellings. But it's slightly different, because here the [isnad] isn't from Malik. So we have four different people narrating this Hadith from Malik.", "The main thing I want you to be aware of here is this is after like one minute of research. This isn't me spending weeks, days, whatever, going through Hadith books; this is after one minute of research. These are who I found narrating this Hadith.", "There's no reason to assume [Bukhari] fabricated this Hadith. Now what someone can say is: maybe [Nafi'] fabricated the chain, or the report. If you're going to be a reasonable Hadith rejector, you're gonna have to say something like that, but you cannot say something like [Bukhari] fabricated the Hadith. And by the way, even in this example, from the one minute of research, [Nafi'] is the only person narrated from; however, it's very likely that this Hadith is actually narrated by others.", "Hadith number 17: the prophet peace be upon him said: love for the ansar is a sign of Faith, and hatred for the ansar is a sign of hypocrisy. My aim is to show you that these hadiths, they're not 250 years late or anything like that.",), "Farid Responds, “Refuting the Claim: Hadiths were Fabricated 250 Years Later”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Q-h8L0-qS4g", ((166, "2:46–3:50"), (240, "4:00–8:38"), (561, "9:21–9:49"), (689, "11:29–12:27"), (769, "12:49–13:52"),), id: 2078)
// U FR091:288-518 | Farid Responds | 5
#passage(("Here we got: Muslim narrating it from [Yahya bin Habib], from [Khalid bin Harith], from [Shu'bah]. Muslim is also narrating it from Muhammed bin [al-Muthanna], from [Ibn Mahdi]. This is what we have: we have [Bukhari] quoting from two narrators, we have Muslim quoting from two narrators, we got [Nasa'i] quoting from one narrator, and all of these narrators, all four of them, are quoting [Shu'bah], and [Shu'bah] is quoting [Abdullah], and he's quoting [Anas]. This is definitely not the only wording of this Hadith. What I've presented to you is one wording of the Hadith; there are other wordings of the same Hadith. This Hadith is narrated by multiple companions.", "There's no way you can say that this is a Hadith that's fabricated [by Bukhari]. The only reason anyone would say something like this is because [Bukhari] is the most famous name. That's the general way that people look at these things. But as we can see here, there's no reason to make this assumption. What's the purpose of this? Because after this, we say: all these hadiths predated [Bukhari].", "This one isn't even Hadith: we said, now we will eat our [fill] of dates, because before the battle the sahaba were extremely poor, and they were really struggling. Finally they'll be able to actually eat properly. It's almost identical: the difference between the two chains is the teacher. Apart from that, it's the exact same thing. We have this additional chain that corroborates. The thing that I want to get across about this specific Hadith is that it's actually just a statement by [Aisha]. If it was a Hadith, you'd find more people quoting it.", "The thing I just want to leave you with is this: if anyone makes this claim that this specific Hadith is 200 years late or whatever, all you got to do, in most cases, you just go to sunnah.com, you type out the Hadith, and you'll find the same in different sources, and you'll find the [isnad] going back to an early narrator. And look in the same chapter, always look in the same chapter, because in the same chapter you're going to find similar reports, usually from different companions. It's just a very easy way of dealing with this.", "I didn't bring the numbers myself; I wanted to test this on the spot with people I didn't know, and there you go: everything that they've mentioned was corroborated. The first two reports were corroborated really well, because they were narrations from the prophet peace be upon him, and the third report is just a statement. So there was a corroboration, but it's not the strongest.",), "Farid Responds, “Refuting the Claim: Hadiths were Fabricated 250 Years Later”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Q-h8L0-qS4g", ((952, "15:52–20:10"), (1265, "21:05–22:53"), (1404, "23:24–27:02"), (1654, "27:34–28:19"), (1706, "28:26–28:52"),), id: 2079)
// U FR051:64-89 | Farid Responds | 3
#passage(("I got two solutions for you. The first solution is something that we trace back to [Umar ibn al-Khattab], the second [caliph] of Islam, a close companion of the prophet peace be upon him. What happened with him was he heard a weird hadith to him, the first time in his life, after the death of the prophet peace be upon him. Abu [Musa al-Ash'ari] called out to him at his doorstep, and he did not receive a reply, and after calling out for the third time he left. So when Omar saw Abu Musa, he said to him: you left. And Abu Musa said, \"Yes, because the prophet peace be upon him said, announce yourself three times and if you're not let in, then leave.\"", "Abu Musa is a very late Muslim. He only converted four years before the death of the prophet peace be upon him. So there's a big difference there. In any case, because of that, Omar had his doubts, and he said: you got to bring someone to corroborate what you're saying. So what Abu [Musa] did is he went to a group, complained about the situation, and they told him yes. And he had a corroboration.", "I recommend you do the same thing. If you find the hadith problematic, you find it weird, you find it strange, all you got to do is run a quick search, and in some cases you'll find that this is something that is reported by other companions, or it's something that's reported by other sources, and this solves a lot of issues for you. Because, for example, you have a hadith in [Bukhari]; you don't like [Bukhari], for example, but guess what? This hadith is in Muslim or Ahmed. So when you see that and you realize this hadith is narrated by multiple people, you have more faith in the hadith. It's not the same as you assuming it only comes through one source. It builds your faith that this is correctly attributed.",), "Farid Responds, “A Simple Solution for Quran Only / Hadith Rejectors”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=61ifU69I_II", ((311, "5:11–5:59"), (375, "6:15–6:43"), (406, "6:46–7:35"),), id: 2080)
// U FR233:100-169 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("This is what you have in mind when someone says I have 30 sources, 50 sources, 100 sources; this is what you have in mind, this image: you imagine 30, 31 sources, they're all quoting the same incident, and these are independent sources. When you go through these sources (and this is not a [Sunni-Shia] thing; this is in general: when anyone is attacking Islam and they're tossing a lot of sources against you, keep in mind that you should go through these sources), this is always the case: whenever someone says here you go, here's 30 sources, 100 sources, it's always the same thing: these are not independent sources. You have 10 of these sources going to source number one, and these sources going to source number two, and these sources going to source number three. So in reality you have three independent sources or something like that; you don't have 30. And that's the game these guys like to play.",), "Farid Responds, “Debunking Shia Claim: Abu Bakr and Omar killed Fatima!! [Mahdi Modarresi]”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u92BmUM3rxM", ((273, "4:33–5:54"),), id: 2081)
// U FR027:194-217 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("These are points directed at my interlocutor, who has made statements in the past. He's made some claims that, to my surprise, can be seen as evidence for the authenticity of hadith, because my interlocutor has said that he agrees with western academics that if you can trace a hadith to a common link, then that report does go back to the common link. So the question is now: why doesn't my interlocutor accept it when the common link is the prophet peace be upon him? He should be consistent, and he should be accepting the common link when the common link is the prophet peace be upon him.", "And here is an example of such. What we have here is a hadith about wiping on the [khuffs], which are like a leather sock kind of thing. What I just shared was just from the six books: we have about 15 from the companions narrating it. In general, you have 41 companions narrating this from the prophet peace be upon him, which means that the prophet peace be upon him is the common link, and therefore this narration is definitely authentic; it's definitely attributed to the prophet peace be upon him.",), "Farid Responds, “Debate: Farid vs MuhammadFromGod - Reliability of Hadith System”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Q3H7g7bsz3M", ((898, "14:58–16:05"), (965, "16:05–17:09"),), id: 2082)
// U FR027:644-659 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("You said that we have very long chains, so we can't rely upon them. But remember, the common link is someone who's early. It's someone from the second generation; it's someone from the first generation. In many cases these are early common links, so it's not going to be a long chain.", "You said [Ibn] Abbas was only 10 when the prophet peace be upon him died. That's fine; he is able to narrate. By the way, [Ibn] Abbas's reports in many cases are corroborated, and not only that, but [Ibn Abbas] will narrate things that he didn't hear himself, but he was taking from the companions of the prophet peace be upon him, so that is sufficient. And at the end of the day, he's a contemporaneous narrator; this is sufficient.",), "Farid Responds, “Debate: Farid vs MuhammadFromGod - Reliability of Hadith System”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Q3H7g7bsz3M", ((3014, "50:14–50:40"), (3051, "50:51–51:25"),), id: 2083)
// U FR091:519-550 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("Let's test the Chinese telephone game. Here is a thing about that: if you have two lines of people participating in the telephone game, let's say there's 20 people in both lines, at the end, if both lines in the telephone game are saying the exact same thing, that means that they were both correct. What you expect is them to have completely garbled up messages, conflicting with each other, having different statements, different rulings, whatever. Instead we find them saying the exact same thing. So it's a very shallow criticism of the Hadith system, but it checks out, because you have them saying the exact same thing.",), "Farid Responds, “Refuting the Claim: Hadiths were Fabricated 250 Years Later”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Q-h8L0-qS4g", ((1742, "29:02–30:06"),), id: 2084)

