#passage(("I'm looking into the first example here that had a three-word match; [in Ahmad] it is reported with a two-word match. So what is it: match the Quran with two words or three? You should be aware that the quran's influence on the narrators is quite large, and due to this sometimes narrator of Hadith will attribute the words to a specific narrator or to a specific companion, and due to the influence of the Quran they may be attributing words to them that are closer to quranic words. It's something that happens because hadiths are usually not narrated verbatim, but they're narrated according to the [meaning].",), "Farid Responds, “Godlogic: \"Someone Matched the Quran!\"”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9QojOL9kr6w", ((340, "5:40–6:29"),), id: 2088)

=== 34.6 Evaluating individual narrators
// NODE P06.C02.S06
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR027:660-713 | Farid Responds | 4
#passage(("The reason why Abu [Hurayra] narrated many records is because he lived for a long time. Abu [Hurayra] died around the year 60, so he lived 50 years after the prophet peace be upon him. It's because he outlived the vast majority of the companions. That's why he became a major narrator of hadith.", "You said that Abu [Hurayra] fabricated a report. I can't remember the exact wording, but he was commenting upon a report, and he says two statements: one is a prophetic hadith, and then he provides some commentary. So someone asks him: what is this? Is this from you? He said yeah, meaning the end part, that commentary, that's what I said. And this is something that we do all the time. We might recite a Quranic verse and then end that with some commentary. Of course, it's hard for it to happen to us, because we're not going to be speaking in a very eloquent classical Arabic or anything like that. But if you say a hadith and you comment upon it, people may think: is that a part of the hadith? No, no, those last three words, that's just me adding something.", "The classical early scholars did do their job. When there was someone that was seen as a suspicious person, someone that was seen as someone that has problem in his character, they would avoid, in general, even if it was someone that met the prophet peace be upon him. One example of this: it is said that there's the verse, it is said that it was revealed about him. There's no hadiths by him; people didn't narrate from him. So if there was suspicion about someone, they would just avoid them. And the vast majority of narrations, as you are aware, they're from the upstanding, well-known, appreciated companions that you and me are familiar with.", "[Abu Hurayra] actually has characteristics of someone who is humble, who rides on a donkey and says: open the paths for the king. He's making fun of the situation. He was a humble guy; he was not someone that let power get to him, even though he was the vice representative of Medina at the time. [Abu Hurayra's] reports are corroborated, and he was not accused of lying by Aisha. Aisha's criticism is: why do you narrate so much in a short space? [The Prophet] didn't just randomly recite reports like that, bam bam bam. But as a teacher he was reciting report after report after report, having people listen to 20 reports at a time. She did not accuse him of lying. Omar actually had [Abu Hurayra] as his representative on [Bahrain]. This is after the fact, after: why are you coming with these horses? Where do you come with these horses from?",), "Farid Responds, “Debate: Farid vs MuhammadFromGod - Reliability of Hadith System”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Q3H7g7bsz3M", ((3092, "51:32–51:54"), (3114, "51:54–52:55"), (3189, "53:09–54:14"), (3260, "54:20–55:30"),), id: 2089)
// U FR027:630-643 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("We look into the evidence. Ali Medini actually went through the reports, through methods that you find being implemented by Western historians today, orientalists, Harold [Motzki]. Medini noticed the differences in [Ibn Ishaq's] report. Sometimes he would say, \"My teacher said.\" Sometimes he would say, \"A man told me my teacher said.\" Sometimes he'll put two people between him and his teacher. These are all signs of reliability, and that's one way in which [Ibn Ishaq's] reliability was proven. So the major critics of hadith, not people that had beef with him like Malik, the major critics of hadith didn't see him as a liar or a [dajjal], as Malik said. And yes, people sometimes do have beef with one another. Just because beef exists between people doesn't mean that everyone's a liar, or everyone should be rejected, or hadith should be rejected as a whole.",), "Farid Responds, “Debate: Farid vs MuhammadFromGod - Reliability of Hadith System”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Q3H7g7bsz3M", ((2944, "49:04–50:12"),), id: 2090)
// U FR027:1647-1677 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("You do realize that what I'm presenting, the Islamic view of historical criticism, is stricter than that. It's stricter than what you're presenting. Even you're saying: why can't you guys be like Omar? Why can't you just look for one corroboration? You guys are making it more complicated.",), "Farid Responds, “Debate: Farid vs MuhammadFromGod - Reliability of Hadith System”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Q3H7g7bsz3M", ((6867, "1:54:27–1:54:48"),), id: 2091)
// U FR027:1061-1098 | Farid Responds | 5
#passage(("You said [Zuhri] was a bottleneck for hadiths and he's politically inclined. Actually, [Zuhri] used to laugh at [Banu Umayya]. If we're expecting [Zuhri] to be this major fabricator of political hadiths [for Banu Umayya], where are they? Where are these reports?", "You said there's a report admonishing that was authentic and that became weak. You need to be specific. You didn't provide evidence [of] anti-[Mu'awiya] report being authentic and then became weak.", "Political issues led to the fabrication of reports? Yes, without a doubt, there are some political issues that led to fabrication of reports, but the vast majority of reports have nothing to do with politics. The vast majority of the reports have to do with details in regards to the prophet peace be upon him telling us to perform salah, [wudu] in a specific way, cleanliness, things like that, things that have got nothing to do with politics. Actually, the clear, direct, heavyhanded political reports, those are usually rejected as fabrications by the scholars of hadith.", "You said that Sunnis rely on Sunnis only. No, that's incorrect. The Sunnis rely on people from different innovations if they are reliable, if they're people that are truthful, if there are people that are trustworthy, if there are people with a high batting average and barely get anything wrong. It doesn't matter if you're someone with a problem in his theology, because at the end of the day, what they are looking for is a truthful narrator, and that's why they relied on the person that you've mentioned and highlighted.", "You said that we submit to the first three generations. Actually, the scholars of hadith critique narrators from the first three generations. Yes, these are the blessed generations, but it doesn't mean that every person there is reliable, and they were of course open to criticism.",), "Farid Responds, “Debate: Farid vs MuhammadFromGod - Reliability of Hadith System”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Q3H7g7bsz3