#passage(("Someone might say: but these are second generation Muslims, which is really early. Well, even though that's the case, it's not necessary for a second generation Muslim to take from a first generation Muslim. Ibn [Hajar] himself mentions that there are some cases in which a second generation Muslim will narrate from another second generation Muslim, down six or seven times. So if there's a single one of those narrators who is anonymous or who's unreliable, he simply can't rely on the report. If the second generation Muslim is not mentioning his source, he simply can't rely on him.", "Now, in regards to this idea of [mursal] reports, and can they be relied upon: yes, there were some Muslims in the past that have relied on [mursal] reports. However, according to the scholars of hadith themselves, they did not rely on these reports; these were tossed away. Of course, I don't mean literally like tossed away; I don't mean they're rubbed out of books; I don't mean they're literally scrapped. I mean they're not relied upon.", "However, those that did rely on [mursal] reports: did they really rely, or were they just being lenient and accepting of those specific reports, because those reports fit in line with their rulings of jurisprudence? That seems to be the case, because when they are faced with the reports that conflicted with their jurisprudential views, they would chuck them out, and they would be refuting them. Those that are interested in practical examples of that: Ibn [Hazm], he actually lists out examples of early scholars that were lenient towards [mursal] reports, unless it was a [mursal] report that kind of conflicted with them; then they would say: we can't rely on [mursal] reports.", "However, the scholars of hadith, they were simply against [mursal] reports in general, and as [Ibn al-Salah] again: there's a consensus in regards to this idea. Of course, objectively speaking, if we're going to be rational, you can't rely on a [mursal] or a disconnected report, simply because you don't know who narrated it, you don't know the source of it, you don't know if it's connected to the prophet peace be upon him or not, if it comes from a liar. And that's why we have to be careful and not accept disconnected reports.",), "Farid Responds, “Origins of the Satanic Verses”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GGkG_cqGnLU", ((238, "3:58–4:33"), (273, "4:33–5:00"), (300, "5:00–5:51"), (355, "5:55–6:28"),), id: 2095)
