#passage(("The first thing is, it's in Sunan Abu Dawud; it's not in Bukhari.", "Ubayd Allah bin Abdur Rahman bin [Rafi]: that specific person, we don't know who he is. There's four or five different opinions about his identity, and due to that you'll find Sheikh Abdullah Saad, who's one of the top experts in Hadith these days, classifying the narration as weak. And his claim actually fits in with the evidence, because we actually don't know who this person is.", "Here are the five names of the narrator that have been mentioned. We have Abdullah bin Abdullah bin Rafi. We have Ubaidullah bin Abdullah bin Rafi. We have Abdullah bin Abdurrahman bin Rafi. We have Ubaidullah bin Abdurrahman bin Rafi. And we have Abdurrahman bin Rafi. So that's five different opinions as to the identity of this person. And in Bayan al-Wahm by Ibn Qattan, he says, \"Whoever he is, he's unknown.\" This isn't a modern critique. This is a classical critique against this narrator.", "If there are five different opinions about the identity of an individual, you cannot rely on the person. What's Al-Albani's evidence of it being Sahih? We're people of evidence. We don't go just because someone said so. Al-Albani is not infallible. He's not a prophet.", "I always thought to myself: a strange thing for Al-Albani to authenticate this report. I wonder what's going on there, but I never looked into it until today. And I noticed something very interesting about this. If you actually look into his As-Silsilah Sahihah, volume two, page 111, he's actually referring to a different Hadith with the same narrator, this Abdullah bin [Rafi] or Ubaydullah bin Abdurrahman bin [Rafi], whatever his name is. He's speaking about a different isnad, and he says, \"This isnad is reliable, with the exception of Ubaydullah bin Abdullah bin [Rafi] Al-Ansari, who is anonymous, and he is the narrator of the Hadith of Bi'r Budha'ah,\" of the well of Budha'ah. So Al-Albani himself recognizes that this narrator is unknown. So what is going on here?", "When you look into Al-Albani's other works, he actually goes into it in more detail. Al-Albani, when he's saying the Hadith is sahih, he's not referring to this specific variation of the Hadith that speaks about the well of Budha'ah, that was a well in which you'd find dogs and whatever and dead inside. He's not referring to that specific report. He's referring to the general report that water is something that is pure, unless water is stained by something that caused the smell to change or the color to change, or the taste to change; that's when you can't use it. But apart from that, when water is water, it's considered to be pure. And that was what Al-Albani's going for. He's not going for the Hadith of Bi'r Budha'ah as narrated by Ubaydullah bin Abdurrahman, or whatever his name is.", "And subhanallah, I guess what my mistake was: I was too hasty in my assumption about Al-Albani. I just looked at the evidence, and I assumed that Al-Albani didn't see the evidence, but no, he did see the evidence. He does consider this strand of the report to be weak.",), "Farid Responds, “Confronting Author on \"The Satanic Bukhari\" Book!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0TwjlL4ui_o", ((266, "4:26–4:31"), (312, "5:12–5:44"), (352, "5:52–6:26"), (449, "7:29–9:45"), (591, "9:51–10:49"), (649, "10:49–11:53"), (713, "11:53–12:10"),), id: 2102)

