#passage(("In my response video to [Yasir], I said that if you're having a discussion with a Christian academic, all you have to do is point out that the report is correctly attributed to a second generation source, and that second generation source is a common link, and multiple people are quoting that second generation source, and then the Christian academic that you're speaking to is going to be obligated to accept the report. Why? It's because Christian academics rely on second generation sources like Luke and Mark. They were not from the first generation. They were from the second generation.", "Here's what we're dealing with: how we get this information from Jesus, peace be upon him, and from Muhammad, peace be upon him. All of this stuff comes from manuscripts. These books didn't fall from the sky. They came in the form of manuscripts. You have usually multiple manuscripts to a specific book of hadith. You have hundreds if not thousands of manuscripts that go back to [Bukhari]. You got a lot that go back to Muslim. And we have hundreds of books of hadith; these books of hadith, they quote early narrators of hadith, usually referred to as a common link. A common link is when you have multiple people quoting one specific person who's known for distributing the hadith. In many cases, that's someone from the second generation.", "And it's so easy to prove this. And that's why you have academics, these are skeptical academics like [Juynboll] and [Schacht], they say: yeah, this person is second generation, he narrated this hadith, and this hadith is correctly attributed to him. This is a skeptical opponent of Islam who attacks Islam. They will admit that these second generation narrators did narrate these hadiths. However, what they'll say is, \"Hey, we don't trust him quoting the first generation narrator. He most likely lied about the first generation narrator.\" That's pretty much it.", "In regards to the second generation narrator, between him and Muhammad, peace be upon him, is one person. So this is good. This is great. If all historical works were like this, we'd be in a much better place. It's so rare when you have a second generation source quoting a report that happened a generation ago. But we have what we have here in the Gospel of Luke. Luke is second generation. But the problem with Luke is that Luke is quoting someone. We don't know who Luke is quoting. That person may be quoting Jesus. It's possible that person never met Jesus. [Robert] Spencer is saying that Luke is a student of Paul. So what? Paul never met Jesus. The information about the life of Jesus, if it's coming from Paul, then it's disconnected. This cannot be seen as authentic if you're going to use the Muslim standard, because the Muslim standard says you need to rely on eyewitnesses.", "Now in terms of the manuscript tradition, the manuscript tradition from Luke is mainly from the third and fourth century. Robert Spencer says, \"It's written really soon. It came really soon after the time.\" Luke, how soon? Two, three centuries. Now, that's what we find with Muslim manuscript tradition. A lot of it's two to three centuries. But Mr. Robert Spencer here, to him, that doesn't work with Muslims. If Muslims have texts that come two, three centuries later, then you can't use them. But he's got no issues using them for Luke. That's a double standard.", "Sometimes, by the way, the common [link] will be the first generation narrator. So I just stuck with second generation. The reason I mentioned second generation is because you have skeptical critics of Islam that will admit that sometimes the common link is a second generation, and they attribute the hadith to the second generation. If Luke is good enough for them, then the hadith is definitely good enough for them. And if they say it's not good enough for them, that's a double standard.",), "Farid Responds, “Robert Spencer's Holes in the Narrative”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o9pYs3mmOX0", ((90, "1:30–2:10"), (240, "4:00–4:57"), (299, "4:59–5:35"), (352, "5:52–7:06"), (435, "7:15–8:07"), (516, "8:36–9:18"),), id: 2106)
