#passage(("Historical reports are not really treated in the same way that hadith reports are treated, because of course hadith reports often include matters that are really tied to our religion, while historical reports sometimes don't, mostly don't. So we find saying that we need to be lenient with historical reports.", "First of all, that's one view; that's not the view of all historians. You have major historians like [Ibn Khaldun] holding the other view, holding the view that even historical reports need to be scrutinized in the same way that hadiths are scrutinized.", "Now in regards to the view that says we can be lenient: what does lenience mean, and what is this lenience directed towards? So generally, the way I like to put it is, there are two things that we have to keep in mind in regards to the way we authenticate these historical reports and determine their authenticity. The first happens to be related to the level of weakness, and the second has to do with the report itself and what is the significance of the report.", "Now how lenient can we be? Imam Ahmed isn't saying, \"Hey, we can be really lenient, we can narrate from liars.\" He's saying take history from [Ibn Ishaq]. Now [Ibn Ishaq] isn't an incredibly weak narrator or a [liar] or anything. He's a mid-level narrator; he's not a bad narrator or anything like that. On the other hand, you have scholars like [Ali ibn al-Madini]: when speaking of historians like [al-Waqidi], he says, \"I won't take genealogy, genealogical data, let alone hadith.\" He was classified as a liar. It doesn't matter if he's [narrating] historical matters.", "So when scholars say let's be lenient with historical reports, they never mean let's accept the narrations of liars when it comes to historical reports. I'm not aware of any scholar that says something like that. If you see a scholar defending the reports of someone who is a liar in historical matters, you usually find that that scholar himself doesn't regard the historian to be a liar, but rather it's his specific view: he views him as someone who's maybe not too trustworthy, so he's lenient with that historian.", "So what about matters that have to do with the significance of the report? It depends on the report itself. Some historical reports are very tied to [aqidah]; they're quite tied to key matters. So let's say, for example, it's a report that has to do with Abu Bakr or something like that: this you have to be strict with. However, if it's a matter that has to do with something like the banner of the Muslims when they went to the battle of [Badr], who cares? That plays no role in how we practice our religion. If it had something to do with the names of the leaders of the battalions or flanks, that doesn't really play such a big deal either.", "However, if these historical reports have some major significance in the way we perhaps take our religion, then we have to be strict. So let's say, for example, if you have a major historical figure who's portrayed in a very negative light, and that historical figure is hated by a political faction or a religious sect, then, if you have an anonymous narrator in the chain or if you have a disconnected report, naturally you would assume that perhaps the anonymous narrator fabricated this report in order to make this historical figure look bad. So we have to take these things into consideration. We can't paint all the historical reports with the same brush; each case needs to be studied on its own.", "But the general rule, I definitely agree: we should be more lenient with historical reports, unless the report has to do with the [aqidah], or a matter that has to do with the way we perceive historical figures, and there is a reason to believe that this report was concocted or manipulated in order to make them appear to be negative. Also keep in mind the opposite could happen: you could have a report attributed to historical figure in order to make him look larger than life: this historical figure performed this and that miracle. Now you can be like, \"Oh, that's a historical report, let's be lenient with that.\" Now you can't really do that, because by doing that, with that leniency, you've just turned a positive historical figure into someone who's saintly. And that's the issue with excessive lenience in historical reports. So you have to keep an eye open for these things.", "Not everything in early Islamic history books are authentic. We generally have to look into the chains of narrations before making a final judgment. But yes, I personally believe that we can be lenient to a degree, unless there is something of significance in the report. And of course we can't be completely lenient, but we can be lenient, again, to a degree; that does not mean relying upon the historical views of liars.",), "Farid Responds, “Can we be Lenient with Historical Reports?”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Vvs-ylOpbKE", ((17, "0:17–0:51"), (56, "0:56–1:16"), (76, "1:16–1:57"), (117, "1:57–3:27"), (217, "3:37–4:12"), (255, "4:15–5:37"), (338, "5:38–6:34"), (394, "6:34–7:45"), (467, "7:47–8:35"),), id: 2110)
// U FR222:108-136 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("When discussing a topic like [al-Jamal], and any controversial historical narrative, historical topic, I always have to quote Sheikh Mohbel, because he says we have to apply the methodology of the [muhaddithin] when speaking about these controversial topics in history. Because if we don't, then we're stuck with every innovative sect using reports that they like, that appeal to them. The [Ibadi] will use this, the [Shia] will use this, the Sunni will use this, and everyone's going to say, \"History says, the authentic reports say this.\" Most people will say this; they don't even know what they're saying, they don't know what they're quoting. Everyone's appealing to his view of history, and what's his view? Whatever suits his beliefs.",), "Farid Responds, “The Battle of al-Jamal (the Camel)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bbpf_VANFAI", ((256, "4:16–5:22"),), id: 2111)

