#passage(("Who are we referring to in the first place? Are those the folks that we're referring to, the conspiracy theorists, revisionists? Because if that's the case, then you don't even need to review them, because other academics have refuted them. It will not have any effect at all, because the lay Muslim doesn't read academic articles put out by western academics, and those with a background in hadith sciences, they're not fooled by the garbage that the skeptical western academics put out.",), "Farid Responds, “Yasir Qadhi Doubts Hadith System?”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qC4fW_789-s", ((77, "1:17–2:10"),), id: 2112)
// U FR046:60-147 | Farid Responds | 5
#passage(("[Motzki] has argued that there is material historical to the time of the prophet, peace be upon him, as well as [Ibn Abbas]. This is just one strand. [Motzki] was definitely one of the better academics. He wasn't one of those from the skeptical camp, and he utilized methodologies like classical, proper hadith scholars. He utilized the same methodology in order to determine that the narrator is actually reliable. The extreme skeptical academics like [Schacht] and [Juynboll] would attribute reports to folks like [Zuhri].", "Yas admitted that the ICMA methodology partially validates many hadith. ICMA is [isnad cum matn] analysis. Now, this is contradicting what you said earlier: now you're saying that it validates many hadith. Alhamdulillah, this is a step in the right direction.", "So what do you mean, that it cannot be proven academically? Because when a [kafir] watches your video, they understand that what you mean is that the academic criteria is based upon objective principles and the isnad system is faith-based. If you do not mean this, then just come out and say it: say that the academic standards are severely biased and flawed.", "If I'm going to present something to the academy, here's what I'd say. I would say this is from [Bukhari], Muslim as well. It can be found in an earlier source like Ahmed. In all these reports, they're all going to one common link, and this report can be traced to him. And perhaps this report is corroborated by someone else. And you're being unreasonable by critiquing the single strands, because it's not necessary for there to be multiple strands everywhere for you to attribute a report to an early narrator, especially if the narrator isn't a big deal, especially if he's not famous for narrating this hadith, especially if he's someone that doesn't have many students.", "And then if they say, \"Well, let's say there's no corroboration,\" then you say, \"It doesn't matter if there's no corroboration, because he's a second generation narrator, and between him and the event is just one person, and that's sufficient for the average historian.\" The average historian would accept that. But if you're dealing with academia that reject a second generation narrator, then if it's a Christian, you tell them, then you got to reject the gospel of Mark, you got to reject the gospel of Luke. And if he's an atheist, say, just reject all history. Don't treat Islam with one standard and treat other works and other civilizations with another standard. They do this; you got to point it out.",), "Farid Responds, “Yasir Qadhi Doubts Hadith System?”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qC4fW_789-s", ((330, "5:30–7:04"), (446, "7:26–7:54"), (538, "8:58–9:21"), (693, "11:33–12:28"), (748, "12:28–13:25"),), id: 2113)
// U FR101:1-134 | Farid Responds | 6
#passage(("We're going to be dealing with 21 arguments by Oxford scholar Dr Joshua Little, and his critiques of Hadith. The reason why it's 21, it's because the intention is to give you so much; it's to overwhelm you. He even admits in the beginning of the video that the first seven aren't even strong arguments; they're the most trivial arguments. And that's the intention: it's just to give you so much that you don't know how to deal with it. If it was just, \"Hey, I'm going to give you the top seven arguments,\" then many people will look into it, will put out responses, whatever. But it's 21; it's going to take you a very long time to go through the whole thing. That's the first point.", "Second point is: people have asked me, why don't you put a response out to this video? I always say, which specific point bothers you? Which of the 21 points have been giving you doubts? I'd never get an answer to that. It's because there's not one specific point that's an issue. It's just, \"Oh, 21 points!\" Is there a problem here? There's nothing; it's just the claim. It just feels heavy; that's the issue, because there's so many arguments.", "By the way, there aren't that many contradictions within [the hadith] material, so to speak. There are some, and you can find lists.", "Imam Ahmed is asked specifically: \"I asked [Abu Abdullah], how many [did Ibn] Abbas narrate saying 'I heard'?\" So he said 10 hadiths. So Ahmed isn't quoting hadiths in which [Ibn Abbas] is saying \"the prophet said\" or \"the prophet ordered\", but he's specifically counting the hadiths in which [Ibn Abbas] is saying \"I heard the prophet, peace be upon him, say.\"", "Yes, contaminations can exist. You can influence someone to think in a specific way; people can be affected by what you've claimed to see in regards to a specific event. It's possible. But is this something that's specific to Hadith? No, this is something that can be applied to all history. So it seems like Joshua's issue is with history; it's with testimony in general; it's not just with Hadith, it's with everyone.", "When he was talking about the [pious] people: earliest book that contains this quote is Muslim, and Muslim says, after quoting, that the lies are being mentioned on their tongue; it's not that they intend to lie. But even if we assumed, let's assume that they're lying, then they would no longer be trustworthy pious people, because they are fabricators of Hadith.",), "Farid Responds, “Refuting Oxford Scholar Dr. Joshua Little on Hadith”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BhmuMn8cxxg", ((0, "0:00–1:00"), (60, "1:00–1:40"), (118, "1:58–2:06"), (177, "2:57–3:42"), (241, "4:01–4:42"), (349, "5:49–6:28"),), id: 2114)

