// U ML120:412-451 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("The thing with Hadith is this: it's not like the Quran. It requires a lot of contextualisation, because an incident can happen, and a Narrator can only narrate part of this incident. He can forget the context, and he can forget the rest of the statement that was said. He can just report the part that he found relevant. So when we combine Narrations, we bring other Narrators completing the context. And also Narrators can narrate with meaning. Sometimes they can give a meaning, but when you have another Narration, you realise what the meaning actually intended is: the Prophet didn't exactly use that specific word, he used a similar word. But when you combine the Narrations, you realise it. And then you have other Narrations mentioning context: OK, so this is in that context, and this is referring to this specific thing. And that's why it takes years.", "But these things I'm telling you, I don't just open that book of Hadith and I find all of these things. No! I have to open the books of explanations of Hadith, and I have to study them. I have to go to \"Fath alBari\" by Ibn Hajar, for example, who explains \"Sahih alBukhari\". I have to go to \"the Nawawi Sharh of Sahih Muslim\", where he explains all of these Narrations that are in the book, all of them, and he will combine the different Narrations. Each book of Hadith has an explanation like this. And that's how we come to an understanding and conclusion, based on years of study and dedication. So that's why approaching Hadith with a lens of just reading it in isolation will always, of course, bring problems. Not to mention the weakening of the Narrations and all of that.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Romanian Christian Grills Muslim With Difficult Questions! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RCeSQlWHPxI", ((1199, "19:59–20:50"), (1252, "20:52–21:29"),), id: 2118)
// U ML001:78-113 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("What you need to understand, and not only in Islam but just in theology in general: you do not take something out of its general context. You do not take one verse or part of a verse and then say, \"Oh, it says this,\" and take it out of the entire corpus of understanding. There's a reason we have tens of thousands of hadith narrations. There's a reason we have one hadith in a multitude of words. Nobody who does theology will agree with doing things this way with any scriptures.", "I'm not talking about interpretation. I told you there's a narration, multiple narrations, about Jewish people following the antichrist [in] the end times. This hadith is talking about specifically the end times. I didn't bring anything from me. It's talking about the establishment of the hour, the end times thing. Every Muslim scholar understands it this way. This is a narration about the end times. In the end times, the antichrist comes. They follow the antichrist. And then there are narrations about Muslims fighting the antichrist followers. None of what I brought you is interpretation. All of this is text. So all you need to do is to bring the text together, and then things make sense.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Confident Agnostic Confronts A Muslim About The Treatment Of Jews In Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IzncpPt4QVo", ((244, "4:04–4:52"), (295, "4:55–5:34"),), id: 2119)

