#passage(("This is narrated by [Yahya ibn] Jaffar, from Abu [Mu'awiyah], from Ibrahim, who is [al-Taymi], from his father, from Abu Dharr. He said: I entered the mesjid and the prophet, peace upon him, was sitting down. So we have an idea of where this is happening: this is occurring in Medina. And it says: when the sun had set, the prophet, peace be upon him, said, \"Abu Dharr, do you know where this goes?\", referring to the sun, and Abu [Dharr] said, \"Allah and his apostle know best.\"", "Now this is a very significant statement, and there is much that we can take from these words, because this event occurred after the revelation of [Surat al-Kahf], after the verses that came down about the sun setting in the murky spring. So the question is: why didn't Abu Dharr say, \"Oh well, the sun goes into a murky spring\"? The companions that were there in the masjid never say that. We only have them saying, \"Allah and the messenger know best\", instead of answering the question that they were supposed to have an answer for according to the Quran. What does this mean? It means that they didn't take the sun going into a murky spring literally.",), "Farid Responds, “The Prostration of the Sun in Islam”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gXGMBM1dP2o", ((19, "0:19–0:52"), (52, "0:52–1:44"),), id: 2138)
