#passage(("The addition in the hadith is due to the narrator's misunderstanding of the Quranic verse and then adding it into the hadith.", "[Ridvan] sees this hadith as silly because of what he projects upon the prophet, peace be upon him. In his mind, the [sun] probably grew a pair of hands and legs in order to prostrate. [Ridvan] thinks that modern science was able to disprove this understanding. But to be honest, this was not the correct understanding in the first place. Of course, this begs the question: how does the sun go under the throne? And the answer, as you can assume, is that it is a metaphysical matter. The throne is not observable, nor is the sun's prostration observable. So, don't expect NASA, let alone [Ridvan], to debunk this using science.",), "Farid Responds, “[DEBUNKED] The Sun Sets in a Muddy Spring at Night”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bO5BYhVOh_0", ((554, "9:14–9:21"), (634, "10:34–12:05"),), id: 2142)
// U FR154:48-226 | Farid Responds | 7
#passage(("The prophet said it goes and asks permission to prostrate. Another interesting thing to keep in mind is: why is that specific time mentioned? Because you have non-Muslims that prostrated to the sun at that time, and this could be the significance behind this whole report. You see, you had the mushrikeen from Arabia, of course not all of them, I'm speaking about specific tribes, they would literally prostrate towards the sun, and at that specific time the prophet is saying: oh, by the way, it's the sun that's prostrating to Allah, and it's the [sun] that is subservient to Allah and recognizes Allah as the [sole] creator, and the [sun] is just a creation of Allah. So that's the significance of the timing.", "As for the nature of this prostration, we don't know. Naturally the sun doesn't have arms and legs and head or anything like that. And of course there are verses in the Quran that speak about the [stars] prostrating, about trees prostrating. We just know that the creation of Allah prostrates to Allah, and in a way that we are not aware of. Of course, the companions didn't understand the prostration of trees to be a physical action that they would observe, and similarly the prostration of the sun isn't in a way that they could physically observe.", "This conversation is interesting. The sun asks permission to prostrate, and one day it is as if being ordered to return [whence] it came; then it will rise from the west. This statement in Arabic says it is as if it is being said to return from where you came. So this is referring to the end of times. In the end of times the sun is going to return, meaning it will be coming from the west. We will witness the sun coming from the west. So there are two ways of understanding this. We could understand this to literally mean the sun itself is going to literally be going around the earth and coming from the west and rising from the west due to the motion of the sun; that's one understanding. The other understanding is that the earth will actually turn clockwise instead of counterclockwise, and that will cause us to view the sun as if it is coming from the west.", "The narrations also, through different paths, but also through Ibrahim bin [Yazid], you also have them add that this occurs under the throne. Now the question is: are we supposed to take this literally or not? Of course, now I'm speaking from the perspective of a non-Muslim that's assuming that the earth is flat: according to this narration, how does the sun go under the earth and under the throne to prostrate? That conflicts with verses, it conflicts with the hadith. The throne of Allah is above the heavens and the earth; it's not under the heavens or the earth. And due to that, the concept of the sun going under the earth in order to get under the throne conflicts with Islam. When Abraham said, \"I am going to my lord, he will guide me\", he did not mean it literally. Similarly, the [sun] going to the throne or going to Allah doesn't need to be taken literally either.", "As for where is the sun when it's prostrating: well, the sun is where it is. Every single day it's moving in its own orbit. It's not prostrating while under the earth, because the throne isn't under the earth, as established in many verses in the Quran and in hadith. And as for the sun going and coming back, this is our perception, and the report itself says it is as if it was said to the sun: go back.", "But let's, for the sake of the argument, say that this report has nothing to do with perception and that this report should be taken literally. Would that be a problem? I'm sure many of you have heard critics of Islam ask the question: where does the sun go at night? Now, the reason why this question is so often asked is because of this specific wording of the hadith: \"Do you know where the sun sets?\" So it is from this statement that they asked where does the sun set at night. Now there is a reason to assume that the word \"sets\" was not uttered by [al-A'mash]. In this other report by Abu [Mu'awiyah], there is nothing about the sun setting; instead it simply asks: where does the sun go? Now, is this unscientific? Is the sun stationary? Because according to modern science the sun is literally moving, and this is in line with hadith. Now, does the hadith say that this is going to occur every night? No. So what exactly is the problem? Well, the sun is moving in its orbit and is heading in the direction of the throne. It's as simple as that.", "Abu [Mu'awiyah] is the closest student of [al-A'mash]. [Abu Nu'aym], on the other hand, was not a close student of [al-A'mash] at all. Furthermore, other students of [al-A'mash] narrate this hadith without saying the words \"sun sets\", including [Sufyan al-Thawri]. Now, this is also the wording of Muhammad [bin Ubayd] in Muslim, another student of [al-A'mash].",), "Farid Responds, “The Prostration of the Sun in Islam”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gXGMBM1dP2o", ((107, "1:47–2:46"), (173, "2:53–3:37"), (220, "3:40–5:02"), (302, "5:02–6:06"), (379, "6:19–7:10"), (430, "7:10–8:22"), (508, "8:28–8:50"),), id: 2143)
// U FR373:76-109 | Farid Responds | 3
#passage(("The full version can be found in [Bazzar]. The report says that the sun goes into a muddy spring and then prostrates under the throne of Allah. Soon I'll explain why the first part of the hadith, which speaks of a muddy spring, is a false attribution to the prophet, peace be upon him.", "Let me show you how this is done. The prophet, peace be upon him, narrated the hadith to [Abu Dharr]. Abu [Dharr] narrated to Yazid bin Sharik. Yazid narrated to [Ibrahim]. The hadith of Sufyan bin Hussein from [Ibrahim]: this is the one that speaks of the sun setting in the murky spring. Elhakam is outnumbered 5 [to 1], and therefore this wording is rejected as an addition. It should be noted that I do not personally believe that this is a misattribution to [Ibrahim] from [Hakam]. Rather, it's more likely that this is from his student Sufyan, who has been criticized by some scholars of hadith. Also, none of the students of [Hakam] narrated this hadith from him except Sufyan, which makes it likelier that this is a mistake from Sufyan.", "Let's assume for a moment that [Ridvan's] addition is correct. That would mean that the sun at the end of a long day soaks into a muddy spring. While the sun is bathing, it finds the throne of God under the water and then starts to prostrate towards it. Now the issue with this ridiculous understanding is that the throne of Allah is not underwater, but it is above the heavens. More importantly, the throne is larger than the heavens and earth. So to conclude, the addition in this hadith strongly contradicts established aspects of Islamic ideology.",), "Farid Responds, “[DEBUNKED] The Sun Sets in a Muddy Spring at Night”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bO5BYhVOh_0", ((345, "5:45–6:02"), (411, "6:51–8:01"), (492, "8:12–8:58"),), id: 2144)
