#passage(("This hadith is the prophet explaining to us the verse in the Quran. That's why the prophet quotes it in the end of the narration. And the verse doesn't talk about sunset or sunrise. So the [sahabi] is asking the prophet where is the sun going. Prophet is not going to say, \"Oh, wait, there's something called general relativity, which you guys don't know\", 1,400 years ago, \"and actually there's this model where the sun is going.\" He's not going to do that for a [Bedouin] Arab 1,400 years ago. The [Bedouin] Arabs asked a basic question. The wise people will give an answer to the extent of understanding of the person asking the question. So prophet now is talking about the explanation of the verse of the Quran, where it talks about the sun moving and going.", "The setting and the rising is in our perception as human beings. If it is referring to our perception, you cannot apply it to reality. When it talks about it rising or setting, it is still moving. We perceive the rising and setting. Until today we say rising and setting. You and me, we say the sun is moving and it's rising and setting. There's no contradiction between the two statements.", "Who cares what the person understood? We're talking about reality. The hadith is not talking about whether the person understood it this way or understood it that way. There's nothing wrong with the wording. This is all I need to present as a Muslim. Is there a problem with the wording of the Islamic text? No. If someone misunderstands some text, we don't blame that on the text. Is there anything in the Quran that tells you go and try to work out what the people in the past understood?",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Atheist Confronts Muslim On Mistakes Found In The Quran! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=L-Zd3evV1oY", ((3446, "57:26–58:12"), (3527, "58:47–1:00:02"), (3609, "1:00:09–1:01:00"),), id: 2145)
