#passage(("[Ridvan] quotes only a part of the hadith instead of the whole hadith in its entirety, making it seem as if the prophet, sallallahu alaihi wasallam, was harshly punishing people who only stole a bunch of camels.", "As for the practice of drinking camel urine, several points should be made here. First of all, the camel urine healed the people who were sick. Therefore, it was effective advice that worked. Second of all, the prophet, sallallahu alaihi wasallam, medical advice is not divine revelation, as explained by Sunni scholars. Thirdly, urine can contain certain types of medical benefit. For example, the urine of a mare is used for making [Premarin], a medicine that is used [for] hormone replacement therapy, which is related to problems with a woman's menopause.", "Due to the nature of changing environments, what might be medically effective in one place or period of time might not be later on, when conditions of a nation or community change. Much of scientific knowledge is derived inductively and based on the observation of the present reality before us, which is susceptible to change and new discoveries that can undermine or improve previous theories and conclusions. So in regards to the urine, all of this can be summed up by saying the urine helped the diseased people, but that does not mean that the prophet, sallallahu alaihi wasallam, medical advice is infallible. Our conditions always change, and consequently so do our methods of survival.",), "Farid Responds, “[DEBUNKED] Mohammed: The First Medical Doctor”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DeHb-Fxx7iY", ((128, "2:08–2:19"), (153, "2:33–3:06"), (189, "3:09–3:47"),), id: 2148)
// U FR146:74-161 | Farid Responds | 5
#passage(("These guys have no idea how to understand the text, because they're assuming that this is referring to the scattering of camels. The report is saying that the camels were stolen. David is affirming that they drank the camel urine, but instead of getting better, they [stay] sick. The report does not include any specific reasons for their apostasy. But David has come to the conclusion that they apostated because the camel urine was something that they were made to drink. David leaves out the part in which they kill the camel herder, and moreover they did not steal the camels in his rendition of the story. But the prophet, peace be upon him, hunts them down and kills [them] in this brutal fashion for their apostasy only.", "Now, this is a ridiculous way of approaching history. If you're going to accuse a narrator of manipulating a report, you can't trust the report in its entirety. You can't just pick out parts that you like and twist the rest of it in order to suit your biases. So, what you're doing here is you're rewriting history, even though you don't really seem to be realizing it.", "Now, how about I provide an explanation that actually fits in with the reports. A group of eight nomads came to the prophet, peace be upon him, in Medina. The narration of [Anas], which can be found in [Bukhari], also implies that they never sincerely accepted Islam. [Anas] says a group of people spoke of Islam, implying that they spoke with their tongues only and feigned Islam. The hypocrisy of the [Bedouins] is a theme that we find in multiple verses in the Quran, after all. In any case, they awaited an opportunity to present itself, and then they actually got sick and asked for some sort of a solution, and apparently camel urine and milk was prescribed by doctors at the time. Anyhow, [they] were allowed to go to Jama, which is 7 kilometers west of Medina, where they spent their time around the camels, where they weren't under the suspicion of anyone.", "Now during their [stay, Ghatafan], an opposing tribe to Islam at the time, raided and stole away the camels that were located to the north of Medina by around 12 km. When the prophet, peace be upon him, and his men were distracted, the [eight] stole the camels. What are the odds of both of these two groups stealing camels at the exact same time? In any case, Islam wasn't their objective; killing the camel herder was not their objective. Their objective was to steal the camels. So when they were caught, they were killed for killing the camel herder and for stealing the camels.", "Now, it's funny how some people can be so overconfident when they have the text right in front of them, and yet they're unable to access it due to not knowing the original language. So Abu [Abdurrahman] here is saying that the hadith is disconnected. Abu [Abdurrahman] is [al-Nasa'i], the author of the book that you're quoting. I don't care about what a publisher has to say. [An-Nasa'i] is an expert in hadith, a master of the science, an early scholar; on the other hand, we have a modern publisher. But for those that are curious, the meaning of the hadith in general, the general hadith in itself, is authentic through other paths. They're not referring to the exact words that are mentioned here in the report specifically. Then this is my argument: [al-Nasa'i] said it's disconnected. That's it.",), "Farid Responds, “The Camel Thieves and Camel Urine Story”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vD0K4p2lc8I", ((388, "6:28–7:57"), (477, "7:57–8:22"), (522, "8:42–9:34"), (574, "9:34–10:24"), (691, "11:31–13:20"),), id: 2149)
