#passage(("Others like David Wood and [Apostate] Prophet have a completely different perspective, and they've completely twisted this report and turned it into some sort of a joke about scientific miracles. I'm not familiar with a single classical scholar that ever referred to this as some sort of a scientific miracle. Maybe there's something they found on some forums, or some random Muslim YouTuber today said this, but I'm not familiar with this being a historical claim. There's nothing really that special about prescribing camel urine as medicine. It was something that was done in the first century. It was something that was in the books by [Ibn Sina]. It was something that you found in medical textbooks in Europe.", "[Ibn Sa'd] states that they cut off the hand and the leg of the herder and stuck thorns into his eyes. He adds that they stole 15 camels. The prophet, peace be upon him, sends after them. They are caught, and they have their arms and legs chopped off. And [Anas bin] Malik states, \"Thorns were stuck into their eyes because they did that to the camel herders.\"",), "Farid Responds, “The Camel Thieves and Camel Urine Story”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vD0K4p2lc8I", ((133, "2:13–3:26"), (214, "3:34–3:57"),), id: 2151)
