#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR378:57-103 | Farid Responds | 5
#passage(("The hadith explains how the dipping of a fly is not for the purpose of being cured, but rather to prevent disease that the fly carries. One wing contains a disease and another contains the cure for it. The [prophet] advised his companions to dip the fly in so that the cure for the disease can be effective. He was not saying that you should dip flies into your drinks so that you have a better diet or something like that. Whether this is the nature of flies in Arabia nowadays might be different, as different conditions result into different natural occurrences. One thing is for sure: flies do contain a type of bacteria that can be used for making antibiotics. So to assume that flies under certain conditions have a type of nature where they contain a disease and a cure for that disease is not a far stretch.", "Besides, the hadith doesn't say that there isn't any other type of potential for disease being present in the fly. It just says that there's a disease that the fly carries which is healed from a cure that the fly carries as well. Other diseases could be present. I think it is rational to assume that the [prophet] was giving advice as to what to do if a fly falls into one's drink and a person wishes to continue using that drink. And that wouldn't be surprising considering the dangerous nature of an Arabian desert where water is not abundant. And again, I wish to emphasize that it is perfectly fine to assume that it was possible that flies back then were of such nature. They might have changed due to a different natural environment that changed over time, and that's perfectly fine.", "Water is for water: that is, water for performing [ghusl] is obligatory when the ejaculation of water, i.e. semen, occurs. So what is being said here is that when you ejaculate, you're meant to wash yourself. It's really simple as that.", "The [prophet] actually, according to other hadiths, worded it differently, saying that the healing is found in the black [cumin] seed, and not that the whole seed itself is a cure. Another interesting thing to point out is that had it been the case that black [seed] on its own was able to heal everything, then the prophet, sallallahu alaihi wasallam, would have not given advice of camel urine to the [Bedouins] in the first place. This proves that what is being done here is either hyperbole, that the [prophet] is overpraising the black [seed] and is not saying that it is a healing for all diseases literally, or that the seed within it contains potential for healing all diseases, but that simply consuming the seed won't necessarily cure everything except death; perhaps a combination with other ingredients is required.", "Regardless of what type of belief in causality you have, none of this contradicts observable nature. If you believe in natural causes, this hadith is not denying that there's no preceding cause to the crying of a baby that would be external to Satan's influence. The simplest way to answer this is to say that just because Satan might be the apparent cause of crying, that does not mean that there aren't other apparent physical causes, like natural processes that precede a cry. A more detailed response is given by prominent Islamic scholar Ibrahim in the 14th century, who explained this type of metaphysics. There might be physical realities that precede certain events, but there's also metaphysical realities that precede them as well. And to claim that one of these is the exclusive cause of the effect is [fallacious]. Actually, both of these things precede certain events.",), "Farid Responds, “[DEBUNKED] Mohammed: The First Medical Doctor”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DeHb-Fxx7iY", ((329, "5:29–6:12"), (372, "6:12–6:54"), (429, "7:09–7:24"), (486, "8:06–8:50"), (544, "9:04–9:53"),), id: 2154)

