#passage(("You also have this idea: there's a Hadith of the Prophet saying that majority of the women are in the fire. The Hadith doesn't say that, and this is a misunderstanding of the Hadith. The Hadith says the majority of the people in the fire will be women. And saying majority of the people in hellfire would be women is not the same as saying majority of the women will be in the fire. So, if I say, for example, that majority of the people in the class are women, that doesn't mean that majority of the women in the world are in the class. So, this is a misunderstanding of some people.", "And generally, historically, if you look at it, you'll see that women would be more than men, and that's why it makes sense to have more women in the hellfire than men. If you look at the West, for instance, there is a lot more women than men in the West. And if you look at the projected studies, they will tell you in the future will be the same thing: there would be more women than men. Women being in the fire more, it doesn't mean that women are bad, and it doesn't mean the majority of the women are going to be in the fire.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Is Islam A Religion Made For The Men?”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OzxyEO708yI", ((647, "10:47–11:18"), (678, "11:18–11:41"),), id: 2155)
// U ML084:779-798 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("Many scholars have said that in Paradise, there will be majority women, as well. So it's just a realistic thing that is going to happen. But nobody's going to be in Hellfire except if they choose to do evil and disobey God. So, no injustice is happening to anyone. But nothing says more than half of women are in Hellfire. There's no such a thing.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Wife Grills A Muslim With Difficult Questions! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tUX4xfgST_E", ((2377, "39:37–40:02"),), id: 2156)
// U ML013:193-222 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("In that same Hadith, they will tell you the Prophet says they are deficient in intellect and they are deficient in religion. I believe this is not the right way to interpret and explain the Hadith. This Hadith, I believe, is actually praising the woman. How's it praising the woman? Prophet: \"I haven't seen anyone that has the same ability as you to persuade or change the mind of a man who's sensible, man who's intelligent, man who is balanced.\" You are able to drive him crazy. You're able to change his mind. You have this ability as a woman: this man is very sensible, is very balanced, is very stoic, but you're able to take him out of that state. This is a form of praise.", "Then, the idea of deficient: it's not deficient. Allah says in the Quran: the earth, we reduce its parts. The same word is used in the Hadith. So it's a form of reduction. Why is that reduction? It's a reduction of responsibility. The Prophet goes on and explains the Hadith. The reduction of the intellect is of testimony, is a responsibility of testifying. Because in Islam, if I testify and I bear false witness, I'm lashed. So Islam gives you the opportunity that you have another woman testifying with you, to make sure that you are saying things the right way and avoid any punishment of bearing false witness, that maybe you remember something in an inaccurate way. He's honoring you. And there are cases in Islam where a woman, she was even a black slave woman, and her testimony was taken over a testimony of a companion of the Prophet.", "The reduction of responsibility in the religion: where you have to pray as a woman and you have to fast, but you don't have to do that when you're menstruating, because Allah is merciful with you. Allah is honoring you. Allah knows that you [are] in pain and that time is difficult time for the woman. So, Allah is saying: don't pray, don't fast. So, that honoring that Allah and the messenger of Allah is giving, they turn it into something negative.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Is Islam A Religion Made For The Men?”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OzxyEO708yI", ((701, "11:41–12:22"), (745, "12:25–13:13"), (793, "13:13–13:33"),), id: 2157)
