#passage(("How does this approve of marital rape? It's simply saying that it's sinful to refuse a husband's call to the bed if that will anger him. It doesn't say that a woman cannot refuse. The key part is not angering the husband. Besides, the prophet, sallallahu alaihi wasallam, told people not to get angry quite often. In fact, the hadith is a proof against marital rape itself, because had it been the case that the [prophet] was as [Ridvan] describes him, [it'd] be expected that he'd say something on the lines of \"he may force himself upon her\" or something like that. However, no such thing is found in the hadith, and is a proof that refusal is allowed. Refusal while angering one's husband is sinful, but certainly isn't a permission for marital rape. Otherwise, it would have been explicitly explained.", "The term marital rape is a very modern term as well. So, although it isn't found in the books officially as marital rape, the idea of a husband forcing himself upon his wife or vice versa is deemed forbidden and sinful according to most Islamic schools, and classified as [darar], or harm, which is a valid reason for divorce.",), "Farid Responds, “[DEBUNKED] Islam vs Women - Apostate Prophet”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Fu7m4EFyuJc", ((945, "15:45–16:29"), (991, "16:31–16:50"),), id: 2159)
