#passage(("You get some of them saying, \"Islam says that a woman is equal to a dog and a donkey.\" You're like, \"Really? Where is this Hadith?\" So, they go to a Hadith where it says that the woman, the dog, and the donkey share in an attribute: they can cut your prayer. So, a woman, a dog, or a donkey, if they pass in front of you while you're praying, they can reduce the reward of your prayer or they can cut your prayer. So, where does this Hadith say that they're all equal? That is similar to saying: there's a sign on the road that says no trucks, no old people, and no animals; then the truck is equal to the animals is equal to the old person. Or to say: I eat food, the dog eats food, and the elephant eats food. So, we're all equal now, because we all do the same thing?", "So, the Hadith is not saying anything about equality there. If a donkey goes and makes that sound, which the Prophet says it's from the Shaitan, it will also confuse me. So, it's about confusing the person. It has nothing to do with the equality of these types. It's not saying they're equal. It's saying that they all share in an attribute.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Is Islam A Religion Made For The Men?”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OzxyEO708yI", ((813, "13:33–14:16"), (856, "14:16–14:42"),), id: 2160)
// U ML120:279-359 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("Many scholars have explained this Hadith, alQurtubi and others. What does it mean to \"cut\" the prayer? It doesn't mean that it nullifies the prayer; as the scholars said, it means that it decreases the reward of the prayer. Why does it mention these 3 specific things? For a simple reason! When the donkey makes noises, it will distract you during your prayer, if it walks in front of you, and that would \"cut\" your prayer, meaning decrease the reward, because you will be distracted; you're not going to focus on your prayer. When it comes to the dog, it will frighten you. Of course, if a random dog crosses in front of you while you're praying, you're going to get frightened. So, that is going to distract you from your prayer and focus, and it will decrease your reward. If a woman crosses in front of you, it can get you to have ideas, it can get you to have lustful thoughts, etc. You can look at the wrong thing in the middle of your prayer; that can distract you. You can lose some of your rewards, and that would cut the prayer, meaning the focus that you have in your prayer.", "That is why these 3 things are mentioned. But nobody is saying that the 3 things are equated to one another. Most scholars in Islamic history have understood it in that way. The Prophet mentions common things. People at that time didn't have all of these buildings that we have, and clean roads. And that's why there's Narrations about, I think, 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas, and he was passing with his horse while the believers were praying in rows. People at that time would pray in the desert; they would pray anywhere they can. Anything can pass in front of you. Any animal can pass in front of them; anything can happen. So, of course, these things need to be contextualised.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Romanian Christian Grills Muslim With Difficult Questions! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RCeSQlWHPxI", ((896, "14:56–16:05"), (967, "16:07–17:19"),), id: 2161)
// U ML120:252-273 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("I'll show you that this has nothing to do with women. Now, the Prophet told us that, for example, if someone passes in front of you while you're praying, you're supposed to repel them and stop them. And then he said, \"if they don't stop, then fight them to push them\". It says: فَلْيُقَاتِلْهُ (fight them!) فإنَّما هو شيطَانٌ (Because he's a shaytan). It means here, man or woman, whoever is passing in front of you, and he uses the same term, and it calls them a devil or Satan or whatever. This has nothing to do with men or women. It has something to do with this person now who is making an act of disobedience. He's doing the work of Satan. Therefore, he's referred to as Satan in that context.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Romanian Christian Grills Muslim With Difficult Questions! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RCeSQlWHPxI", ((745, "12:25–13:10"),), id: 2162)
// U ML120:100-245 | The Muslim Lantern | 7
#passage(("First, \"shape\": I don't agree with this translation. It's صورة Sura (image), not شكل shakl (shape); these are 2 different words. An \"image\", like an image: تُقْبِلُ في صُورَةِ شيطَانٍ، وَتُدْبِرُ في صُورَةِ شيطَانٍ، فَإِذَا أَبْصَرَ أَحَدُكُمُ امْرَأَةً فَلْيَأْتِ أَهْلَهُ؛ فإنَّ ذلكَ يَرُدُّ ما في نَفْسِهِ. This is what the Narration is saying.", "So, when it says \"In an image of Satan\", there are 2 ways to understand this. There is the way that when people are walking by, and you look at a woman, they see the devil with two horns. Obviously, the two horns are just a Hollywood characterisation. Or that this is metaphorical, referring to something else. Now, any reasonable person would say that this is not saying that when you walk around, and see a woman in the street, that she looks like the image of the devil. Nobody is going to say that. No reasonable person is ever going to interpret the Narration in that way.", "So, what does it mean, then? The \"image\" there is referring to the lust, which is portrayed by Satan, that is trying to get you to do lustful impermissible acts, whether it is adultery, whether it is fornication, or any type of these specific acts, even just touching her, hugging her, or looking at her with lust! Like the Bible says (Matthew5:28), looking at a woman with lust is adultery, etc. So, when it says the \"image\" there, it means the image that Satan wants you to focus on, to look at, to disobey God with, that lust, lustful image that is not permissible for you to look at. Now, nobody else in the Islamic History has ever interpreted that therefore this means that the woman is Satan when she's walking around. So, that is not a fair interpretation in any way, shape, or form.", "Now, when it says (Come to your family), that doesn't mean you should cave to your lust. In fact, that means the opposite. So, this Narration is the cure for a specific type of individual. Not every single person is like that! For an individual, that if he saw and then thought about something: this can happen to anyone! We are not saying that it only happens to people who are not righteous or whatever. No! This can happen to any human being; we're all men. And anybody who claims that it doesn't happen to them is probably lying. So, anybody can look at a woman and get lustful. This is common sense.", "So, in this Narration, the Prophet had intercourse with his wife, and then he said this. So, he's setting an example for the people. That doesn't mean that the Prophet is looking at women lustfully, but he's setting an example for the people of what to do in case this situation arises or takes place. So, any human being can look at a woman lustfully, whether he's married or not married; it can lead him to either fornication or adultery. In this case, it's adultery, because he's married. So, this Narration is specifically referring to someone who is married. So, you're married, and if you get something in yourself and start feeling lustful, that could lead you into that wrong direction, then go and have intercourse with your wife. So, this is a cure for a specific type of problem that can arise sometimes and cannot arise sometimes.", "Now, we cannot take a Hadith that is referring to a specific instance and then apply it to every single case scenario in every human being, and then derive from it that it means that every human being is lustful or whatever. No! Why? I'll give you a very simple answer from the Quran. The Quran itself teaches us to lower our gaze (anNoor24:30): قُل لِّلۡمُؤۡمِنِینَ یَغُضُّوا۟ مِنۡ أَبۡصَـٰرِهِمۡ (And say to the believing men to lower their gaze) وَیَحۡفَظُوا۟ فُرُوجَهُمۡۚ (And to guard their private parts), meaning not to do fornication or anything. So, the Quran tells you to lower your gaze. You will not need to look at a woman anyway in order for you to do this! And the Prophet said: if you looked accidentally, then to look away. So, the Prophet gave us many ways to avoid this.", "But if someone were to look and then feel lustful, etc., and then he starts to feel something: the Prophet is referring to \"something\" now, repelling something that you will get, a certain desire, a lustful desire that can lead you to do something incorrect. Then do this! Because this is permissible for you, and that would repel your desire. And men will know this, that this desire can be very strong, but the minute the man does his need, it disappears. So, that is the most wise thing to say, actually. So, this Hadith is definitely giving a cure to many people, that if some people were to apply, we would have so many less cheating, scandalous relationships in the West, actually.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Romanian Christian Grills Muslim With Difficult Questions! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RCeSQlWHPxI", ((414, "6:54–7:18"), (440, "7:20–7:53"), (477, "7:57–8:43"), (536, "8:56–9:46"), (588, "9:48–10:28"), (634, "10:34–11:18"), (681, "11:21–12:08"),), id: 2163)
