#passage(("The narrators speak about things in detail. For example, someone would say: why does the narrators have to specify that prophet Muhammad had x amount of white hairs in his beard, which is a narration we have? Because our tradition is to be very specific in the description of what the prophet say. In fact we have narrators where if a narrator is doubtful he would say \"or he said this\". So they would be very specific in their descriptions, because this for us is a religion, and the prophet said: whoever lies upon me intentionally, let him prepare his seat in the hellfire. So for us, we have to be very careful when we talk about the messenger of Allah, because this is religion. So the descriptions, whether what their color was or where they were from or whatever, that is very specific, because the narrators are specific in their descriptions of the actions that the prophet Muhammad does, because sometimes there are wisdoms we can get from the details that we may not see but scholars may be able to see. So they narrate all the details in general.", "Now, the second part, about two for one: two for one here has nothing to do with their color. Two for one here has to do with the fact that this slave that came to the prophet, first he came and he did [bay'ah], pledge of allegiance; he accepted Islam, he gave pledge of allegiance. The prophet did not know that he was a slave. Because you gave a pledge of allegiance that you'll fight with the messenger of Allah, you'll protect him, you'll obey the rules, you'll do all of that; but if you are a slave to someone else, you don't have the authority to just leave; you're held to that person. So prophet did not know, and now the prophet felt compelled that he wants to help him, because he already gave him the [pledge] of allegiance. So the prophet wanted to help him. So the prophet wanted to buy him from the other person.", "So now if I want to buy something from you, I'm trying to give you a great offer that you would not refuse. So I want your car for whatever reason (in this case, the prophet want him because he gave him [bay'ah]): I want your car, take two of my cars in exchange of that car, because I want your car and I want to give you an offer that I make sure that you're going to accept my offer. Hence why the prophet gave two in exchange of one. Because here there's nothing about the value of them being less than the value of the others, and you wouldn't find any explanations of any scholars that would say that is because their value is less. All of the scholars will tell you the prophet gave it out of generosity, because he felt now compelled to help the other one that gave [bay'ah]. He felt like: I need to help him. He gave me [bay'ah]. He pledged allegiance. He believes in me. I want to help him. So here, take two for one, two for the price of one as they say, so you can agree to him, because maybe if he is one for one he say: no, I don't want to sell my slave, for example; I don't want to let him go free. So the prophet said: here's an offer that you're not going to refuse; take two for one.", "Of course, we know this idea that the people would belittle individuals based on the race. People would belittle individuals based on their identity; people will belittle individuals based on a variety of different things. [The Prophet] mentioned something that culturally people are belittling, even though Allah and the messenger have clarified that there's no distinction. If you want to look at the hadith, we have to look in a hadith in the corpus of everything that the Quran is going to teach about that topic. [The Prophet], in his last ceremony right before his death, in front of over 100 thousand companions, said to them: there's no superiority for a black over a white, there's no superiority for a white over a black, except with piety, righteousness, a closeness towards God. Allah in the Quran says: we made you into different nations and tribes. The best among you is the one who's more pious, connected to God. So it's very clear in Islam that there is no distinction or belittling based on the race or the country or the individual or the looks, or you're a slave or a free person. Islam is clear on these things.", "However, people still have these things. That's why [the] prophet multiple times talked about cultural things that people had from pre-Islamic times. [The] prophet said to them: \"It's not from us that does this thing from pre-Islamic time.\" Because the prophet is reminding them: you still have these problems, you still have these issues; don't have them, remove them. So the prophet is saying you have to obey your ruler even if you're looking at that ruler in a belittling way, which you shouldn't be doing but is culturally known. So the prophet gave the example because in the culture they will look at that person with a lesser eye than they would other people. So it's actually honoring that person, saying: if that person is your ruler, your leader, you would obey him even if you think in your community that because he's a Ethiopian or he looks like this or he's a slave, that he is someone to be belittled.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Inquisitive Agnostic Grills Muslim On Cousin Marriage And Racism In Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fvVrT_WHQxg", ((57, "0:57–1:50"), (132, "2:12–2:56"), (176, "2:56–3:54"), (262, "4:22–5:21"), (321, "5:21–6:09"),), id: 2172)
// U ML001:8-15 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("There is war in the end times that will happen, and as the narrations say, the Jewish people will follow the antichrist when he comes, the [Dajjal], and believers will fight the disbelievers, and they will be among his army, because they're waiting for their Messiah, and Jesus is the Messiah and he already came. The army of the [Dajjal] will be fought, and Jesus will kill the antichrist.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Confident Agnostic Confronts A Muslim About The Treatment Of Jews In Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IzncpPt4QVo", ((42, "0:42–1:04"),), id: 2173)
// U ML127:589-872 | The Muslim Lantern | 9
#passage(("You said: an old man had a piece of rock. He dropped it. He said: this is enough for me. And he was killed later.", "The first chapters of Sahih Bukhari are: The 1st chapter is about وَحي (wahiy), كتاب الوحي (Kitab alWahiy: Chapter of Revelation). The 2nd Kitab (chapter) is about إيمان (iman: faith). The 3rd is about وضوء (wudu: ablution) that you do before the prayer, and غُسل (ghusl): it's also about washing the body. Then حَيض (hayd: menstruation). Then كتاب التيمُّم (atTayammum), which is something that you do also, in case you don't have water, for you to pray. And then, the book of مَواقيت الصلاة (Prayer times), and then the one of آذان (adhan: the call to prayer). These are the first few books of Bukhari. So when you claim it was in the beginning of the book, where is it in Sahih Bukhari?", "Chain of Narration: that means if you're holding Sahih alBukhari in your hand, it's not just going to tell you \"the Prophet said\". It's going to say: \"it's been reported to us by this person, he told us that this person, he told us that this person, he told us that this person...\" and then he will mention the Narration. It only says: narrated 'Abdullah bin Mas'ud.", "\"Saw him killed as a non-believer\": does it say \"we went and killed him as a non-believer\"? If it's going to be used, it has to have one of two things: either it commands you to go and murder a non-believer, or it mentions that the people did it! So then you'd say: oh, that's a justification, they did it, so I'm going to do it. All the Narration says is the person who narrates, later, saw that person was killed as a non-believer. When it says \"killed as a non-believer\", meaning he didn't accept Islam and became a believer? No! He remained as a non-believer, and was killed, and when he died, he was a non-believer. That's all the reporter is telling you. He's not telling you: we killed him. He's not telling you to go and kill anyone.", "This is the problem: when we have people who are uneducated, and they go to Islamic sources. They read in Islamic source; they don't understand how to navigate these texts. In Bukhari, what he does, for example, he will mention multiple Narrations about one incident. So if you go to the Narration before, the Narration after, you'll see, here, he's talking about prostration, or the verses of Surat anNajm specifically, or these incidents to do with the prostration in Surat anNajm, or the chapters that are going to be talking about prostration. So Bukhari collects separate Narrations about one specific topic. Now what scholars do is that they know the background, they know the context of the event, and then they tell you: the event is one, two, three; this Narration is talking about this specific thing, and this is how it's understood. But when someone takes something, completely disregards it of its Islamic context, of course you're not going to be able to understand anything, because obviously you're taking the thing completely out of the historical context.", "Even an extremist is not going to be able to use this hadith. And that extremist, if he follows the Quran and Hadith \"closely\", then he would read the other Narrations about the topic! He would read the Narrations about not killing anyone unjustly; he would read the entire verses about not doing that.", "Now, what is this talking about? This, here, is referring to a verse, a specific verse in Chapter anNajm, Surat anNajm of the Quran, where a specific verse was revealed (anNajm53:59-61), that Allahﷻ ('Azza wa Jal: Almighty and Majestic is He) was warning the disbelievers, and then Allahﷻ commands people to prostrate, ie. prostrate to your Lord (anNajm53:62). So in the Quran, there's something called ۩ \"place of prostration\" ۩. There are certain verses where a Muslim would prostrate to God after reading this verse ۩, where God commands you to prostrate, or God mentions the prostration ۩. Now, there is a verse in Surat anNajm which mentions prostration. Now, the historical background is: when the Prophetﷺ recited this verse, he recited this verse to the believers, and the disbelievers were there. And due to the fact that the Quran had so much miraculous nature, and eloquence of speech, and they were Arabs, and they are understanding that this is the Speech of God, not a human being, because they knew the Arabic language, so they all fell into prostration reading those verses. This Narration here mentions one person excluded from the prostration from the disbelievers who were listening to the verses of the Quran being recited. That person, the narrator says: I saw him, he did not prostrate, but he took a piece of the dirt, and he put it on his head, and he said: \"that's sufficient for me\". Rather than prostration, he did that. So that was his \"prostration\", essentially. The context is very simple!", "Now, when he says \"I saw him later on killed as a disbeliever\": because there was multiple battles between the Muslims and non-Muslims. So the narrator says he saw him in a battle; there was a battle between Muslims and non-Muslims; he saw that person –that was so arrogant that he didn't prostrate– he saw him dying as a disbeliever in battle. So, there were battles. The Prophetﷺ, for example, when he left Makka– because he was being persecuted for 13 years, his Companions were killed, Muslims were very weak, so the Prophet had to leave and go to Medina, in which he was welcomed. The minute he left, the disbelievers went to fight him. The Battle of Badr was there; there was 1,000 disbelievers and 300 believers. They barely had any weapons, but they still won the battle, and they fought the disbelievers, and many of the heads of Quraysh –the tribe in Arabia– the disbelievers, were killed, in a battle that they started, that they attacked the Muslims. Then Uhud was after it, in which also they attacked the Muslims. Then Khandaq happened, which also, they attacked the Muslims. So this Companion is talking about a person who did not prostrate when a verse of the Quran was revealed, because he was arrogant, and he's telling us the outcome: that he saw that person –because of his arrogance, God punished him– that he died in the battlefield as a disbeliever.", "Now, no extremist will ever be able to use this verse, because at least if those extremists read the Quran and Hadith, they would at least know the context. They're not going to pick this verse, which does not command you to kill anyone, which doesn't say that they killed anyone! They're not going to be able to use any of that and ignore everything else in the text, which is explicitly clear. There isn't a single extremist that uses this as a justification, this specific Narration.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Researcher Confronts Muslim On Extremist Teachings! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=G5Mgp9SzUc0", ((1621, "27:01–27:06"), (1646, "27:26–27:54"), (1744, "29:04–29:36"), (1821, "30:21–32:49"), (1980, "33:00–33:51"), (2037, "33:57–34:23"), (2068, "34:28–36:00"), (2161, "36:01–37:15"), (2241, "37:21–38:02"),), id: 2174)
