#passage(("There is a narration in [Abu Dawud] in which the prophet peace be upon him makes a judgment upon a Jewish man and woman, and rules upon them with what is found within the [Torah]. Now, in this Hadith the report includes an addition in which the prophet peace be upon Him says: I believe in you and he who revealed you. He's saying this to the [Torah], after placing it upon a cushion. The specific report is graded as [hasan], meaning a [mid-tier] Hadith.", "So what do we do in this situation, when different Scholars disagree with each other? They're both established Hadith Scholars, and they both [graded] a massive number of hadiths. So what we do in this situation is we stick with the evidence. The discussion revolves around a narrator called [Hisham], who [Albani] considers to be a mid-tier narrator. Of course, these decisions in regards to how strong or how weak a narrator is basically revolves around what classical Scholars have had to say about those narrators, and we often find classical compilations that list out the opinions of the classical Scholars of Hadith. And yes, you will find a lot of criticism about him, but you will find a bunch of quotes that seem to put him in that mid-tier category. So I can definitely see why [Shu'ayb] arrived at his conclusion; I can also understand why [Albani] arrived at his conclusion. However, there are some works that perhaps weren't visited by [Albani]. And now, despite [Abu Dawud] himself saying that [Hisham] is one of the strongest narrators from [Zayd bin] Aslam, it's still drown in the sea of criticisms.", "Moreover, there's another criticism, which is that we also have the same report, and they don't include this addition. I'd also like to add that we have the same report from Abdullah: he also doesn't mention this part about the prophet peace be upon Him placing the [Torah] on the cushion and saying I believe in you. And this supports the classical Muslim position.",), "Farid Responds, “[Debunked] The Prophet Accepted the Torah”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mTYGcnF8UQ", ((100, "1:40–2:14"), (134, "2:14–4:00"), (242, "4:02–4:46"),), id: 2175)
