// U FR161:1-118 | Farid Responds | 8
#passage(("This is a response to a conspiracy theory that was brought forward by Dr. I don't feel like this is a point that's even worth responding to, because it's so ridiculous. There are books, late books that say he foams in the mouth. So, the words are very similar. Dr. is claiming that the correct word is meaning foaming and not meaning the color changed, and there's a conspiracy that occurred in which [Sahih] Muslim was tampered with, and that's why modern versions of [Sahih] Muslim say so.", "The response to this is that this is not a modern change in Muslim. In fact, you can find classical manuscripts of [Sahih] Muslim that include the word. For example, Rashid Pasha in Turkey has it, and this is a copy that was written in the 8th century. Furthermore, five of the earliest manuscripts of Muslim, none of them include the word.", "Here is how you find out if there's something wrong in a specific edition of a book. It's quite simple. All you do is you try to find other editions of the same book. Just by this alone we can come to the conclusion that the edition that Dr. Jalin relied upon included a mistake. This is most likely due to the editor of that specific edition that he was quoting from.", "Another method of determining whether a specific word is correct is by looking at the word in the other works of the same author. For example, his commentary on Muslim itself: he clearly defines it, and then explains the word as a changing of the color of the face.", "Always look at the source. Ignore what he's claiming. Look at the source itself. We see here there's no indication that the author is even quoting Muslim. So he can't claim that this author is pointing out something in Muslim that is no longer there. This guy, he doesn't even know that Muslim is a name. And now he wants to talk about conspiracy theories involving a cover up that has to do with Muslim.", "[Karl Pfander] had a hidden copy of [Sahih] Muslim? He passed away 150 years ago. These manuscripts predate him by like five, six centuries. He's using a source by a missionary in order to argue that [Muslim's] been tampered with.", "Another method to determine the correct wording in a book is by looking up the manuscript yourself. This is a manuscript of the same book from the University of Princeton.", "There are at least three ways of determining whether a specific word should be in a text or shouldn't be in a text, and what's the correct wording that's supposed to be in a text. One is by comparing multiple editions of the same book. Two is by looking up different books by the same author. And three, it's by returning to the original manuscripts themselves in order to determine what is the proper word that's supposed to be used.",), "Farid Responds, “Saheeh Muslim Conspiracy Uncovered!!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TrJPXAS1MRA", ((3, "0:03–2:03"), (123, "2:03–2:39"), (280, "4:40–5:20"), (326, "5:26–5:57"), (362, "6:02–7:41"), (494, "8:14–8:30"), (525, "8:45–8:58"), (541, "9:01–9:33"),), id: 2194)

=== 36.4 Weak hadiths in the collections and books of fabrications
// NODE P06.C04.S04
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR112:1-78 | Farid Responds | 3
#passage(("These are three really common questions that I often find in comments, I often find on Twitter. The first question that usually comes up is: why do you still have weak hadiths in your classical works? Why aren't these hadiths removed? Well, they're not removed because that would be a betrayal of the author, one, and it will be a betrayal [of] the trust of readers, too. The reader wants the original work by the author, so if you're going to take things out, then you're going to be cheating the reader. That's the first point. But hypothetically, even if narrations were removed from classical Works, what critics of Islam would do is they'll be like: aha, you guys are manipulating your own hadiths, you're manipulating your own Works, shame on you Muslims. And again, it would be a betrayal, so that's why it doesn't happen.", "What does happen, though, is you will have footnotes that indicate whether a Hadith is authentic or weak. That's a very good solution. Another thing that you will sometimes find is you'll have a new version of a work, and clearly on the title it will say version of this work, so you know what you're getting, you know what you're buying. For example, about 15 years ago or something, the editors focused on Gathering the authentic narrations and printing that right on its own. They also did another section where they collected the weak reports. You do not want to manipulate or tamper with the works of classical [scholars]. That's not something that decent people do. If they're going to make any sort of change, you're going to have to include in the title. The Reader needs to know what they're reading, what they're getting, what they're buying.", "Of course, one would be asking: why do they even have weak hadiths in the first place? Multiple reasons. In many cases they include weak hadiths to strengthen stronger hadiths, or to strengthen Hadith that is already established. Sometimes they collect weak hadiths to show you that it's a weak Hadith, and you'll find many works that clearly State, like [at-Tirmidhi] for example: he'll mention a Hadith and he'll say this Hadith is actually rejected. Sometimes they'll quote a Hadith in order to prove to you that a narrator is a liar. So they have loads of reasons why they collected weak hadiths as well.",), "Farid Responds, “Three Common Claims about Hadith”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=otvYP10qsDo", ((3, "0:03–1:11"), (75, "1:15–2:42"), (164, "2:44–3:23"),), id: 2195)
// U FR353:1-93 | Farid Responds | 4
